<http://example.org/tgs#Interview.TGS_10_NoraBateson> a owl:NamedIndividual ;
    <http://example.org/thinkr#discusses> <http://example.org/tgs#Concept.KleibersLaw> ,
    <http://example.org/tgs#Concept.Schismogenesis> ,
    <http://example.org/tgs#Concept.Superorganism> ,
    <http://example.org/tgs#Concept.Symmathesy> ,
    <http://example.org/tgs#SchoolOfThought.WarmData> ;
    <http://example.org/thinkr#hasCanonicalSource> <https://www.thegreatsimplification.com/episode/10-nora-bateson> ;
    <http://example.org/thinkr#hasEpisodeSegment> <http://example.org/tgs#EpisodeSegment.MainShow> ;
    <http://example.org/thinkr#hasEpisodeType> <http://example.org/thinkr#EpisodeType.Interview> ;
    <http://example.org/thinkr#hasGuest> <http://example.org/tgs#Persona.NoraBateson> ;
    <http://example.org/thinkr#hasHost> <http://example.org/tgs#Persona.NateHagens> ;
    <http://example.org/thinkr#hasTranscript> <https://www.thegreatsimplification.com/wp-content/uploads/2022/03/TGS10NoraBatesonTranscript.docx.pdf> ;
    <http://purl.org/dc/terms/contributor> <http://example.org/tgs#Persona.NoraBateson> ;
    <http://purl.org/dc/terms/creator> <http://example.org/tgs#Persona.NateHagens> ;
    <http://purl.org/dc/terms/source> <https://www.thegreatsimplification.com/episode/10-nora-bateson> ;
    <http://example.org/thinkr#hasDiscussionSummary> "hasDiscussionSummary ADDED 2026-08-13 from MJSullivan's own uploaded transcript — this entity had been otherwise fully built without one. Nora's real, foundational thesis is stated directly in the opening minutes: culture is highly skilled at naming and measuring 'the parts' of any system, but far less able to perceive the relationships between them — a real, pointed distinction she illustrates with a family, which can't function like a machine where a malfunctioning 'part' gets simply swapped out. Her explanation of symmathesy is offered as a real etymological construction, not a borrowed term — sym (together) plus the Greek mathisi (learning) — coined specifically because she felt systems thinking lacked a word for transcontextual mutual learning across an ecosystem's members. The conversation's most vivid moment is Nora's own real 'dog shit hill' metaphor: two paths up a hill, one direct but covered in waste, one longer but clean — offered as a direct challenge to the presumption that the 'efficient' linear path is actually efficient once its full real cost is counted. Her real explanation of schismogenesis distinguishes two of her father Gregory's original types (complementary, where an escalating dominance/submission gap eventually erases the relationship itself; symmetrical, where escalating competition between equals can remain creative even as it consumes everything else in the relationship) plus a third, real type she attributes to a Finnish research group rather than her father: 'systems holdback,' where each party withholds from a relationship in direct proportion to what they perceive the other withholding. A real, genuinely vulnerable disclosure closes the conversation's personal register: Nora describes deliberately raising her own children with systems-thinking and complexity skills, only realizing afterward that she hadn't anticipated their peers lacking the same tools — leaving her kids cast into real, unwanted roles as 'the helper, the healer, the rock' for people around them who were themselves struggling. The episode's real closing image, offered in response to Nate's question about energy redundancy, reframes infrastructure itself as a relationship problem: Nora's own anecdote about a friend who builds laundromats specifically because that's where isolated people are forced to talk to each other, extended by Nate into a real, direct principle both land on together — 'every single one of our basic needs should have a community around it.'"@en .

<http://example.org/tgs#Interview.TGS_126_AVisionForBetterment> a owl:NamedIndividual ;
    <http://example.org/thinkr#discusses> <http://example.org/tgs#Concept.CarbonPulse> ,
    <http://example.org/tgs#Concept.DarkTriad> ;
    <http://example.org/thinkr#hasCanonicalSource> <https://www.thegreatsimplification.com/episode/126-daniel-schmachtenberger-7> ;
    <http://example.org/thinkr#hasEpisodeSegment> <http://example.org/tgs#EpisodeSegment.MainShow> ;
    <http://example.org/thinkr#hasEpisodeType> <http://example.org/thinkr#EpisodeType.Interview> ;
    <http://example.org/thinkr#hasGuest> <http://example.org/tgs#Persona.DanielSchmachtenberger> ;
    <http://example.org/thinkr#hasHost> <http://example.org/tgs#Persona.NateHagens> ;
    <http://example.org/thinkr#hasReplay> <https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=tmusbHBKW84> ;
    <http://example.org/thinkr#hasTranscript> <https://www.thegreatsimplification.com/wp-content/uploads/2024/06/TGS126DanielSchmachtenbergerTranscript.pdf> ;
    <http://purl.org/dc/terms/contributor> <http://example.org/tgs#Persona.DanielSchmachtenberger> ;
    <http://purl.org/dc/terms/creator> <http://example.org/tgs#Persona.NateHagens> ;
    <http://purl.org/dc/terms/source> <https://www.thegreatsimplification.com/episode/126-daniel-schmachtenberger-7> ;
    <http://example.org/thinkr#hasDiscussionSummary> "Opens with real, direct continuity from a prior conversation — Nate frames this explicitly as 'kind of the first followup' to an earlier discussion on AI and the superorganism roughly a year prior. Schmachtenberger's real core distinction, stated with unusual precision: naive progress achieves a narrowly-defined goal while externalizing harm elsewhere, then generates a self-congratulatory narrative from survivorship bias — told by 'those that happen to make it through,' not the losers of wars, the extinct species, or the colonized. He is careful to characterize this generously as naive, and less generously as 'propaganda and apologism for winners.' A real, extended historical section traces this pattern through named figures (Genghis Khan credited with roughly 40 million deaths, 11% of the world's population at the time) directly alongside real counter-examples of pre-modern constraint-design systems — the Iroquois Confederacy and Pacific Northwest tribal fishing rules cited as real, working precedents for building limits into a system's own structure rather than relying on individual restraint. The conversation's most concrete, quantified section covers PFAS contamination directly: a real cited $10.3 billion 3M settlement, a real single-state remediation cost estimate of $50 million, and a real, striking claim that removing all PFAS at current production rates would cost roughly $200 trillion annually — about twice global GDP. A real, pointed institutional critique follows: the 14th Amendment's corporate-personhood application, with a real, specific historical claim that by 1912 the Supreme Court had heard 28 cases on the rights of African Americans against 312 on the rights of corporations under the same amendment. Schmachtenberger's own closing framing is genuinely reflective rather than prescriptive: he proposes 'yellow teaming' — tracing an action's full network of causal effects before building it, using a healthy tree's cascading positive externalities (to soil, water, insects, other species) as the real model for what well-designed action looks like — and closes by explicitly cautioning against rushing into action, advocating instead for 'a deeper movement into withdrawal' and honest reckoning with how much of the impulse to act quickly is itself unprocessed ego or avoidance. Nate's own closing response is genuinely personal and unscripted: rather than summarizing, he says he's going to go sit by a tree at dusk and reflect."@en .

