<http://example.org/tgs#Interview.TGS_138_FritjofCapra> a owl:NamedIndividual ;
    <http://example.org/thinkr#discusses> <http://example.org/tgs#Concept.Superorganism> ;
    <http://example.org/thinkr#hasCanonicalSource> <https://www.thegreatsimplification.com/episode/138-fritjof-capra> ;
    <http://example.org/thinkr#hasEpisodeType> <http://example.org/thinkr#EpisodeType.Interview> ;
    <http://example.org/thinkr#hasGuest> <http://example.org/tgs#Persona.FritjofCapra> ;
    <http://example.org/thinkr#hasHost> <http://example.org/tgs#Persona.NateHagens> ;
    <http://purl.org/dc/terms/contributor> <http://example.org/tgs#Persona.FritjofCapra> ;
    <http://purl.org/dc/terms/creator> <http://example.org/tgs#Persona.NateHagens> ;
    <http://purl.org/dc/terms/source> <https://www.thegreatsimplification.com/episode/138-fritjof-capra> ;
    <http://example.org/thinkr#hasDiscussionSummary> "A genuinely warm first meeting despite decades of parallel intellectual work — both Nate and Capra directly express real surprise at how closely their independently-developed worldviews converge, with Capra's own closing words naming a 'fundamental, conceptual, maybe even spiritual kinship' he hadn't expected walking in. Capra traces his own real intellectual origin with unusual precision: reading Heisenberg's 'Physics and Philosophy' as a Vienna undergraduate, understanding 'only about half of it' at the time, yet carrying the book's core insight — that subatomic reality resists the language physicists had available to describe it — through his entire subsequent career. A real, substantive structural connection surfaces directly in the show notes: Capra's own term 'Economic Superorganism,' independently arrived at, sits alongside this graph's own already-built Concept.Superorganism as a genuine point of real convergence between the two thinkers' frameworks, not a borrowed term. Capra's own real 'four characteristics of life' (self-organizing networks that are regenerative, creative, and intelligent) are offered directly to graduate students and postdocs as a real, actionable research program — reflecting on what these characteristics imply for their own specific discipline, regardless of field. The conversation closes on real, unhedged political directness: Capra's own 'magic wand' answer is to eliminate money from politics entirely, describing US institutional corruption as structural rather than a matter of individual character — no politician, in his own words, can reach high office today 'without being corrupt,' not due to personal flaws but because the system itself requires it."@en .

<http://example.org/tgs#Interview.TGS_146_BillPlotkin> a owl:NamedIndividual ;
    <http://example.org/thinkr#hasCanonicalSource> <https://www.thegreatsimplification.com/episode/146-bill-plotkin> ;
    <http://example.org/thinkr#hasEpisodeType> <http://example.org/thinkr#EpisodeType.Interview> ;
