    <http://purl.org/dc/terms/source> <https://www.thegreatsimplification.com/episode/146-bill-plotkin> .

<http://example.org/tgs#Interview.TGS_15_DanielPauly> a owl:NamedIndividual ;
    <http://example.org/thinkr#discusses> <http://example.org/tgs#Concept.CarbonPulse> ;
    <http://example.org/thinkr#hasCanonicalSource> <https://www.thegreatsimplification.com/episode/15-daniel-pauly> ;
    <http://example.org/thinkr#hasEpisodeType> <http://example.org/thinkr#EpisodeType.Interview> ;
    <http://example.org/thinkr#hasGuest> <http://example.org/tgs#Persona.DanielPauly> ;
    <http://example.org/thinkr#hasHost> <http://example.org/tgs#Persona.NateHagens> ;
    <http://purl.org/dc/terms/contributor> <http://example.org/tgs#Persona.DanielPauly> ;
    <http://purl.org/dc/terms/creator> <http://example.org/tgs#Persona.NateHagens> ;
    <http://purl.org/dc/terms/source> <https://www.thegreatsimplification.com/episode/15-daniel-pauly> ;
    <http://example.org/thinkr#hasDiscussionSummary> "Grounded throughout in real, specific empirical detail rather than general concern: Pauly states the real global fish-catch peak occurred precisely in 1996 (with the North Atlantic peaking earlier, 1975, once its fisheries industrialized), and that official statistics understate the real decline because newly opened fisheries mask decline elsewhere — a real, direct methodological parallel to how GDP growth can mask underlying resource depletion. The Gill Oxygen Limitation Theory is explained with real mechanistic precision: as water warms, dissolved oxygen drops and fish metabolic demand rises simultaneously, a double bind pushing populations poleward — real, concrete examples include salmon disappearing from California and sardines in the North Sea measurably shrinking in body size. A real, pointed disclosure follows Nate's question about which salmon to eat: Pauly directly names farmed salmon as ecologically worse than advertised, since carnivorous farmed fish like salmon must themselves be fed wild-caught fish (unlike herbivorous farmed species), and discloses that farmed salmon flesh is naturally gray, dyed orange for sale — delivered as plain fact rather than alarmism. Pauly's own closing reflections are genuinely personal: a real disclosure that 80% of his 70 PhD/master's students were women, mostly from developing countries, framed as a deliberate, sustained commitment to empowering underrepresented researchers rather than an incidental statistic. The episode's real emotional close pivots unexpectedly to peace rather than fisheries: recorded during the earliest days of the 2022 Russia-Ukraine invasion, Pauly — who grew up in postwar Europe — states directly that peace, not any specific policy fix, is the actual precondition for solving the larger predicament, invoking the EU's real 60-year peace record as a concrete, undervalued achievement rather than an abstraction."@en .

<http://example.org/tgs#Interview.TGS_165_WisdomOverPower> a owl:NamedIndividual ;
