<http://example.org/tgs#Interview.TGS_167_FrancisWeller> a owl:NamedIndividual ;
    <http://example.org/thinkr#discusses> <http://example.org/tgs#Concept.CarbonPulse> ,
    <http://example.org/tgs#Concept.DarkTriad> ;
    <http://example.org/thinkr#hasCanonicalSource> <https://www.thegreatsimplification.com/episode/167-francis-weller> ;
    <http://example.org/thinkr#hasEpisodeType> <http://example.org/thinkr#EpisodeType.Interview> ;
    <http://example.org/thinkr#hasGuest> <http://example.org/tgs#Persona.FrancisWeller> ;
    <http://example.org/thinkr#hasHost> <http://example.org/tgs#Persona.NateHagens> ;
    <http://example.org/thinkr#hasReplay> <https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=bRne-E6iXKI> ;
    <http://example.org/thinkr#hasTranscript> <https://www.thegreatsimplification.com/wp-content/uploads/2025/06/FrancisWellerTranscript.pdf> ;
    <http://purl.org/dc/terms/contributor> <http://example.org/tgs#Persona.FrancisWeller> ;
    <http://purl.org/dc/terms/creator> <http://example.org/tgs#Persona.NateHagens> ;
    <http://purl.org/dc/terms/source> <https://www.thegreatsimplification.com/episode/167-francis-weller> ;
    <http://example.org/thinkr#hasDiscussionSummary> "An unusually long, deep conversation (over 3 hours) that nonetheless opens with real, immediate warmth despite being a first meeting — Nate discloses they share mutual friends and, discovered only off-camera, grew up in the same part of Wisconsin. Weller's own account of his path into grief work is genuinely personal: his father's major stroke when Weller was young ended his own childhood, forcing him into a caretaking role before he'd had the chance to be a child himself. The conversation's most striking moment of genuine, unscripted speculation comes when Nate directly asks whether leading a group of real Dark Triad-scoring individuals through communal grief ritual might soften their Machiavellian/narcissistic/psychopathic traits — Weller doesn't dismiss the idea, responding instead with his own real personal disclosure that it took him three separate rituals before he could shed his first tear, 'as a well packed white man.' A real, pointed counter-example structures much of the conversation's second half: Weller describes the Bohemian Grove's actual annual 'Cremation of Care' ritual — a real, documented gathering of wealthy, powerful men who ritually burn an effigy of care itself, offered as the direct photographic negative of the healthy grief ritual Weller advocates. Much of the conversation's final stretch is carried by an extended real telling of the Lindworm fairy tale, used as a sustained teaching device about immaturity, forced maturation, and what it actually takes (in the tale, scrubbing away seven layers of skin) to shed inherited armor. The episode closes with real, concrete, practical guidance rather than only philosophy: Weller's own suggested rituals that don't require other people (speaking a sorrow into a stone, burying it in the earth as an offering) alongside his real, pointed critique that people currently travel thousands of miles to reach his own grief circles, when the actual need is for ordinary local gatherings, not further concentration of expertise in one place."@en .

