<http://example.org/tgs#Interview.TGS_1_RichardGephardt> a owl:NamedIndividual ;
    <http://example.org/thinkr#hasCanonicalSource> <https://www.thegreatsimplification.com/episode/1-richard-gephardt> ;
    <http://example.org/thinkr#hasEpisodeType> <http://example.org/thinkr#EpisodeType.Interview> ;
    <http://example.org/thinkr#hasGuest> <http://example.org/tgs#Persona.RichardGephardt> ;
    <http://example.org/thinkr#hasHost> <http://example.org/tgs#Persona.NateHagens> ;
    <http://purl.org/dc/terms/contributor> <http://example.org/tgs#Persona.RichardGephardt> ;
    <http://purl.org/dc/terms/creator> <http://example.org/tgs#Persona.NateHagens> ;
    <http://purl.org/dc/terms/source> <https://www.thegreatsimplification.com/episode/1-richard-gephardt> ;
    <http://example.org/thinkr#hasDiscussionSummary> "A genuinely personal episode from its opening minutes — Nate's first real question isn't about policy but about why an 80-year-old with a storied career isn't simply retired, and Gephardt's own answer is a real, extended account of his working-class St. Louis childhood: a father whose back injury ended his trucking career, a mother who went to work, and a real, specific formative moment — a high school speech teacher personally arranging a Northwestern summer scholarship that Gephardt directly credits as opening his entire adult path. The episode's real analytical core is Gephardt's own direct comparison between the 9/11 aftermath and January 6th: he offers a real, personal account of running through a small Missouri town on the Christmas after 9/11 and finding every house flying an American flag in solidarity with New York, offered as concrete evidence of genuine national unity — directly contrasted with January 6th, which he attributes to Americans who had been, in his own words, effectively 'brainwashed' by an alternative-reality information ecosystem into believing violence was justified. Gephardt is careful throughout to extend real empathy alongside his critique, stating directly that he understands the mindset that produced January 6th even while condemning the outcome. The episode's closing stretch turns genuinely personal and practical: Gephardt's own real daily routine (two meals within an eight-hour window, a standing bowl of raw vegetables) is directly credited by Nate as something he adopted into his own life as a result of this conversation. The episode closes with real, warm testimony to Gephardt's own reputation — Nate directly relays that mutual colleagues refer to him, half-joking, as 'a saint and a national treasure and a superman' — and Gephardt's own closing words return to a real, concrete personal ethic borrowed from his time as a Boy Scout: leave the campsite better than you found it."@en .

<http://example.org/tgs#Interview.TGS_20_MaximumPowerHyperAgents> a owl:NamedIndividual ;
    <http://example.org/thinkr#hasCanonicalSource> <https://www.thegreatsimplification.com/episode/20-daniel-schmactenberger> ;
    <http://example.org/thinkr#hasEpisodeSegment> <http://example.org/tgs#EpisodeSegment.MainShow> ;
