<http://example.org/tgs#Interview.TGS_213_ChristineWebb> a owl:NamedIndividual ;
    <http://example.org/thinkr#discusses> <http://example.org/tgs#SchoolOfThought.TechnoOptimism> ;
    <http://example.org/thinkr#hasCanonicalSource> <https://www.thegreatsimplification.com/episode/213-christine-webb> ;
    <http://example.org/thinkr#hasEpisodeType> <http://example.org/thinkr#EpisodeType.Interview> ;
    <http://example.org/thinkr#hasGuest> <http://example.org/tgs#Persona.ChristineWebb> ;
    <http://example.org/thinkr#hasHost> <http://example.org/tgs#Persona.NateHagens> ;
    <http://example.org/thinkr#hasReplay> <https://youtu.be/ArHrLzkJm_k> ;
    <http://example.org/thinkr#hasTranscript> <https://www.thegreatsimplification.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/03/TGS-213-Christine-Webb-Transcript.pdf> ;
    <http://purl.org/dc/terms/contributor> <http://example.org/tgs#Persona.ChristineWebb> ;
    <http://purl.org/dc/terms/creator> <http://example.org/tgs#Persona.NateHagens> ;
    <http://purl.org/dc/terms/source> <https://www.thegreatsimplification.com/episode/213-christine-webb> ;
    <http://example.org/thinkr#hasDiscussionSummary> "Webb is careful from the outset to frame human exceptionalism as a specific cultural worldview rather than a species-wide trait — direct, real evidence she offers: it is not correlated with any human universal, and even the book's own title risks implying otherwise. A real, substantive methodological critique threads through the conversation: Webb argues mainstream scientific practice itself often unintentionally reinforces human exceptionalism, and directly cites the Heisenberg Uncertainty Principle as an analogy for how the very act of studying animals in artificial lab conditions can distort what's actually being measured. A real, pointed cross-cultural comparison anchors the conversation's political dimension: Webb and Nate discuss the Netherlands' real political parties explicitly organized around animal interests (Party for the Animals, Peace for Animals) as a live counterexample to the US's own more anthropocentric default, while Webb is careful not to romanticize this, directly noting real, current Dutch immigration tensions in the same breath. The episode's most ethically pointed claim is Webb's own direct assertion of a real causal and correlational link between believing in a strict human-animal divide and holding prejudiced attitudes toward human outgroups — she extends this historically to the real dehumanization of women in ancient Greece and of enslaved people. A real, practical closing recommendation structures much of the interview's final third: Webb's own 'slow looking' exercise (sustained, unstructured attention to another living being — a bird feeder, a rock formation, a pigeon) which she assigns to her own students and reports produces a real, noticeable shift in perception within about a week. The episode closes with real, candid personal disclosure from Nate about his own recent two-month recording break and total knee replacement surgery, and Webb's own closing 'magic wand' answer is offered without hedging: a pill that would induce more humility, awe, and wonder toward the rest of nature."@en .

