<http://example.org/tgs#Interview.TGS_2_ShannaSwan> a owl:NamedIndividual ;
    <http://example.org/thinkr#hasCanonicalSource> <https://www.thegreatsimplification.com/episode/02-shannaswan> ;
    <http://example.org/thinkr#hasEpisodeType> <http://example.org/thinkr#EpisodeType.Interview> ;
    <http://example.org/thinkr#hasGuest> <http://example.org/tgs#Persona.ShannaSwan> ;
    <http://example.org/thinkr#hasHost> <http://example.org/tgs#Persona.NateHagens> ;
    <http://purl.org/dc/terms/contributor> <http://example.org/tgs#Persona.ShannaSwan> ;
    <http://purl.org/dc/terms/creator> <http://example.org/tgs#Persona.NateHagens> ;
    <http://purl.org/dc/terms/source> <https://www.thegreatsimplification.com/episode/02-shannaswan> ;
    <http://example.org/thinkr#hasDiscussionSummary> "A genuinely foundational episode for a real, recurring throughline across this whole graph — this is the real primary source for content directly cited years later in RR-23, RR-15, and TGS_99 (Jeremy Grantham's own direct funding of Swan's research). Swan is careful to preempt an obvious objection early: since conception only requires one sperm, why does a falling average count matter? Her real, direct answer treats sperm count as a distribution, not a threshold — quality and viability requirements mean a large surplus is needed to reliably produce even one successful candidate, and average counts below roughly 40 million per milliliter are clinically considered subfertile. A real, striking comparative policy discussion follows: Denmark's approach, which pairs fertility incentives with real, sustained public attention to environmental exposures, is credited by Swan with actually halting decline in long-term study data — directly contrasted with several Asian countries she names as offering only financial incentives without addressing exposure, which she states plainly 'is not working.' Nate offers a real, speculative extension live in conversation, drawing a direct parallel between a hypothetical future where only a small fraction of fertile men father most children and the real historical case of Genghis Khan's Y-chromosome persisting in a notable percentage of East Asian and, by some estimates, American men today — offered explicitly as speculation, not as something Swan endorses. The episode closes with Swan's own real, direct appeal: she wants this issue to generate the same public 'buzz' climate change has achieved, stating it may be 'perhaps even as serious as that and deeply connected to it' — a real, direct claim about relative severity, not just topical adjacency."@en .

