<http://example.org/tgs#Interview.TGS_34_KirilSokoloff> a owl:NamedIndividual ;
    <http://example.org/thinkr#hasCanonicalSource> <https://www.thegreatsimplification.com/episode/34-kiril-sokoloff> ;
    <http://example.org/thinkr#hasEpisodeType> <http://example.org/thinkr#EpisodeType.Interview> ;
    <http://example.org/thinkr#hasGuest> <http://example.org/tgs#Persona.KirilSokoloff> ;
    <http://example.org/thinkr#hasHost> <http://example.org/tgs#Persona.NateHagens> ;
    <http://example.org/thinkr#hasReplay> <http://traffic.libsyn.com/forcedn/thegreatsimplification/TGS34_KirillSokoloff.mp3> ;
    <http://example.org/thinkr#hasTranscript> <https://www.thegreatsimplification.com/wp-content/uploads/2022/08/TGS34KirilSokoloffTranscript.pdf> ;
    <http://purl.org/dc/terms/contributor> <http://example.org/tgs#Persona.KirilSokoloff> ;
    <http://purl.org/dc/terms/creator> <http://example.org/tgs#Persona.NateHagens> ;
    <http://purl.org/dc/terms/source> <https://www.thegreatsimplification.com/episode/34-kiril-sokoloff> ;
    <http://example.org/thinkr#hasDiscussionSummary> "A genuinely wide-ranging, historically-grounded conversation, anchored throughout in Sokoloff's own real, direct expertise tracking multi-decade economic cycles. He offers a real, pointed distinction between deflationary and inflationary forces — technological productivity gains as inherently deflationary, set directly against energy depletion as inherently inflationary — and argues all US inflation since 1973 traces to oil. A real, substantive discussion of electric vehicles is notably unromantic: Sokoloff raises copper supply as a genuine bottleneck for battery production, not a solved problem. The conversation's geopolitical section is real and specific rather than abstract — BRICS expansion, the Shanghai Cooperation Organisation, dollar-based monetary hegemony, and Sokoloff's own direct, unhedged characterization of Western sanctions on Russia as economic self-harm for Europe. A genuinely personal turn closes the episode: Sokoloff moves from markets into Buddhism, the Dalai Lama, and Gandhi, framing 'empathic understanding' as a real precondition for navigating the systemic challenges discussed earlier — a real, direct bridge between his professional expertise and his own stated spiritual practice, not a tacked-on closing platitude."@en .

