<http://example.org/tgs#Interview.TGS_99_JeremyGrantham> a owl:NamedIndividual ;
    <http://example.org/thinkr#discusses> <http://example.org/tgs#Concept.CarbonPulse> ;
    <http://example.org/thinkr#hasCanonicalSource> <https://www.thegreatsimplification.com/episode/99-jeremy-grantham> ;
    <http://example.org/thinkr#hasEpisodeType> <http://example.org/thinkr#EpisodeType.Interview> ;
    <http://example.org/thinkr#hasGuest> <http://example.org/tgs#Persona.JeremyGrantham> ;
    <http://example.org/thinkr#hasHost> <http://example.org/tgs#Persona.NateHagens> ;
    <http://example.org/thinkr#hasReplay> <https://youtu.be/cTvN9iFJ0fY> ;
    <http://example.org/thinkr#hasTranscript> <https://www.thegreatsimplification.com/wp-content/uploads/2023/11/TGS99JeremyGranthamTranscript.docx.pdf> ;
    <http://purl.org/dc/terms/contributor> <http://example.org/tgs#Persona.JeremyGrantham> ;
    <http://purl.org/dc/terms/creator> <http://example.org/tgs#Persona.NateHagens> ;
    <http://purl.org/dc/terms/source> <https://www.thegreatsimplification.com/episode/99-jeremy-grantham> ;
    <http://example.org/thinkr#hasDiscussionSummary> "One of the longest interviews in the whole podcast (roughly 2 hours 20 minutes) and unusually wide-ranging even by that standard. The clearest, most substantive moment is a real, named intellectual disagreement rather than deference: Nate directly pushes back on Grantham's dismissal of debt as 'shockingly misunderstood and exaggerated in significance,' arguing from a biophysical rather than purely financial lens that debt creation still draws down real resources even if it nets to zero on a balance sheet. Grantham's own response is notable for what it is NOT — he doesn't argue back, explicitly declining ('I don't think it matters... I don't want us to spend any more time') while calling his own position 'eccentric,' a graceful agree-to-disagree rather than resolution by persuasion. Grantham discloses, unprompted, a genuine self-correction on his own public image: despite being introduced as a billionaire, he states he's 'at least an order of magnitude short of that' by any literal measure, only reaching billionaire status by counting money already given away — Nate's own opening had already flagged this correction as something Grantham does in the conversation. The episode's emotional and scientific core is Grantham's direct, personal account of funding Shanna Swan's sperm-decline research and connecting it to a parallel, independently-observed collapse in insect populations (including his own real three-hour conversation with E.O. Wilson) — delivered with real urgency ('we're on our way out of business') rather than abstract concern. The closing 'magic wand' answer is genuinely stark and stated without hedging: Grantham says he would reduce global population to 1 billion, framing the entire modern predicament as a roughly hundred-year mismatch between population growth and technological capacity. Grantham closes with direct, substantial praise for the podcast's long-term potential impact, having 'spent 30 or 40 hours' reviewing prior episodes before this recording, and Nate ends by inviting him back for a future Reality Roundtable."@en .

