<http://example.org/tgs#Monologue.Frankly_123_TheConsumptionPyramid> a owl:NamedIndividual ;
    <http://example.org/thinkr#discusses> <http://example.org/tgs#Concept.CarbonPulse> ,
    <http://example.org/tgs#Concept.Superorganism> ;
    <http://example.org/thinkr#hasCanonicalSource> <https://www.thegreatsimplification.com/frankly-original/123-the-consumption-pyramid> ;
    <http://example.org/thinkr#hasEpisodeSegment> <http://example.org/tgs#EpisodeSegment.Frankly> ;
    <http://example.org/thinkr#hasEpisodeType> <http://example.org/thinkr#EpisodeType.Monologue> ;
    <http://example.org/thinkr#hasHost> <http://example.org/tgs#Persona.NateHagens> ;
    <http://example.org/thinkr#hasReplay> <https://youtu.be/EOmG0B2q9aI> ;
    <http://example.org/thinkr#hasTranscript> <https://www.thegreatsimplification.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/02/Frankly-123-Transcript-The-Consumption-Pyramid.pdf> ;
    <http://purl.org/dc/terms/creator> <http://example.org/tgs#Persona.NateHagens> ;
    <http://purl.org/dc/terms/source> <https://natehagens.substack.com/p/essay-the-consumption-pyramid> ,
    <https://www.thegreatsimplification.com/frankly-original/123-the-consumption-pyramid> ;
    <http://example.org/thinkr#hasDiscussionSummary> "Grounded in an unusually candid, specific personal story: Nate discloses that the idea for this framework came to him during an overnight hospital stay for knee surgery, when a planned stop at a favorite Indian restaurant was thwarted by the restaurant being locked and gated — he later realized this coincided with real ICE activity staging nearby in Minneapolis, prompting a direct, uncomfortable reflection on how his own minor inconvenience (missing dinner) sat alongside potentially much higher-stakes disruption for the restaurant's own staff. Nate is careful and explicit that the pyramid is not a purity test: most people move across several layers within the same week, and he discloses his own real fluctuation directly ('I can have super discipline for three months straight and then slide upward in this pyramid during a stressful week, that's me too'). A real, structural nuance he insists on: the same physical object can sit on different layers for different people depending entirely on context — a smartphone as pure status object for one person, genuine functional necessity for another who depends on it for banking and public services. The framework's real stakes, per Nate's own direct argument, are asymmetric under contraction: higher layers depend on complex, brittle, global systems that don't degrade gracefully, while lower layers tend to be more local, repairable, and substitutable — making intentional descent, before conditions force it, a real form of what he calls sovereignty rather than austerity. The episode closes with a real, direct callback (John Merrick's 'I am not an animal, I am a human being') repurposed as Nate's own closing line — 'I am not a consumer, I am a human being' — followed by a real, concrete request that listeners share the framework with 2-3 others rather than simply consume it alone."@en .

<http://example.org/tgs#Monologue.Frankly_129_DesperatelySeekingAgency> a owl:NamedIndividual ;
    <http://example.org/thinkr#hasCanonicalSource> <https://www.thegreatsimplification.com/frankly-original/129-a-guide-to-staying-human-part-1> ;
