    <http://example.org/thinkr#hasHost> <http://example.org/tgs#Persona.NateHagens> ;
    <http://purl.org/dc/terms/creator> <http://example.org/tgs#Persona.NateHagens> ;
    <http://purl.org/dc/terms/source> <https://www.thegreatsimplification.com/frankly-original/129-a-guide-to-staying-human-part-1> .

<http://example.org/tgs#Monologue.Frankly_132_FrameworkForAction> a owl:NamedIndividual ;
    <http://example.org/thinkr#discusses> <http://dbpedia.org/resource/Planetary_boundaries> ,
    <http://example.org/tgs#Concept.CarbonPulse> ,
    <http://example.org/tgs#Concept.DarkTriad> ,
    <http://example.org/tgs#Concept.DistributedAgency> ,
    <http://example.org/tgs#Concept.GreatSimplification> ,
    <http://example.org/tgs#SchoolOfThought.PlanetaryBoundaries> ;
    <http://example.org/thinkr#hasCanonicalSource> <https://www.thegreatsimplification.com/frankly-original/132-what-to-do-as-the-world-falls-apart> ;
    <http://example.org/thinkr#hasEpisodeSegment> <http://example.org/tgs#EpisodeSegment.Frankly> ;
    <http://example.org/thinkr#hasEpisodeType> <http://example.org/thinkr#EpisodeType.Monologue> ;
    <http://example.org/thinkr#hasHost> <http://example.org/tgs#Persona.NateHagens> ;
    <http://example.org/thinkr#hasReplay> <https://youtu.be/TH8aHQ3Y6Zk> ;
    <http://example.org/thinkr#hasTranscript> <https://www.thegreatsimplification.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/03/Frankly-132-Transcript-What-to-Do-as-the-World-Falls-Apart.pdf> ;
    <http://purl.org/dc/terms/creator> <http://example.org/tgs#Persona.NateHagens> ;
    <http://purl.org/dc/terms/source> <https://natehagens.substack.com/p/essay-what-to-do-as-the-world-falls> ,
    <https://www.thegreatsimplification.com/frankly-original/132-what-to-do-as-the-world-falls-apart> ;
    <http://example.org/thinkr#hasDiscussionSummary> "Nate frames this explicitly and repeatedly as a personal turning point, not just another episode — 'as much a personal rite of passage as it is a Frankly,' gestating for a decade and finally organized under the pressure of real, live world events (the Iran war, unfolding in real time as he records). A genuinely notable rhetorical move: Nate directly names the risk of his own prior 20 years of work — that continued diagnosis without a action framework becomes 'a category error' once the underlying diagnosis is broadly accepted — and explicitly declines to spend this episode on news updates about the Strait of Hormuz crisis itself, redirecting instead to what he calls the only proportionate conversation: what to actually do. The most philosophically pointed moment is Nate's direct, carefully-hedged engagement with anti-capitalist and decolonial critique: he states real sympathy for those values and concedes 'there's much in those critiques that's correct,' but argues explicitly that naming the system correctly is not itself a plan, and that waiting for total systemic transformation as a precondition for any other action means 'waiting forever' — arguing instead for parallel construction, building new structures while the old ones still stand. The Timeline axis is offered as the episode's own 'most important orienting idea' — explicitly not a sequence to complete and move past, but three genuinely overlapping conditions, with Phase C (the stable attractor) framed as impossible to reach by skipping the disruption of Phases A and B, only reachable by building through them. The episode closes on a real, personal reflection on leadership: Nate argues modern systems select for Dark Triad traits (already a real, built Concept in this graph) in leaders, creating adverse selection, and offers a genuinely counter-intuitive closing reframe — that a viewer's own reluctance to lead 'might be a good indication that you could be a good leader tomorrow.' He explicitly declines to resolve the framework's own open question of what to do, insisting that question 'can only be answered by us as individuals,' a real refusal to prescribe despite having just spent nearly an hour building a framework that could easily be read as prescriptive."@en .

