<http://example.org/tgs#Monologue.Frankly_139_Part2FourVariables> a owl:NamedIndividual ;
    <http://example.org/thinkr#discusses> <http://dbpedia.org/resource/Planetary_boundaries> ,
    <http://example.org/tgs#Concept.CarbonPulse> ,
    <http://example.org/tgs#Concept.Superorganism> ,
    <http://example.org/tgs#ScenarioFacet.ACapturedDemocracy> ,
    <http://example.org/tgs#ScenarioFacet.CascadingBreakdown> ,
    <http://example.org/tgs#ScenarioFacet.CivicIdeal> ,
    <http://example.org/tgs#ScenarioFacet.ForcedFeudalism> ,
    <http://example.org/tgs#ScenarioFacet.FriendlyNeighborsWithGoodFences> ,
    <http://example.org/tgs#ScenarioFacet.GreenGrowth> ,
    <http://example.org/tgs#ScenarioFacet.HothouseTriage> ,
    <http://example.org/tgs#ScenarioFacet.MadMax> ,
    <http://example.org/tgs#ScenarioFacet.Mordor> ,
    <http://example.org/tgs#ScenarioFacet.QuietUnraveling> ,
    <http://example.org/tgs#ScenarioFacet.StrainedButWorkable> ,
    <http://example.org/tgs#ScenarioFacet.TheColdWarScenario> ,
    <http://example.org/tgs#ScenarioFacet.TheGeopoliticalDangerZone> ,
    <http://example.org/tgs#ScenarioFacet.TheGlobalizationIdeal> ,
    <http://example.org/tgs#ScenarioFacet.TheGreatSimplification> ,
    <http://example.org/tgs#ScenarioFacet.TheStewardshipDeal> ,
    <http://example.org/tgs#SchoolOfThought.PlanetaryBoundaries> ;
    <http://example.org/thinkr#hasCanonicalSource> <https://www.thegreatsimplification.com/frankly-original/139-how-to-think-about-the-future-part-2> ;
    <http://example.org/thinkr#hasEpisodeSegment> <http://example.org/tgs#EpisodeSegment.Frankly> ;
    <http://example.org/thinkr#hasEpisodeType> <http://example.org/thinkr#EpisodeType.Monologue> ;
    <http://example.org/thinkr#hasHost> <http://example.org/tgs#Persona.NateHagens> ;
    <http://example.org/thinkr#hasReplay> <https://youtu.be/5R0oUGPf9C4> ;
    <http://example.org/thinkr#hasTranscript> <https://www.thegreatsimplification.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/04/Frankly-139-Transcript-How-to-Think-About-the-Future-Part-2.pdf> ;
    <http://purl.org/dc/terms/creator> <http://example.org/tgs#Persona.NateHagens> ;
    <http://purl.org/dc/terms/source> <https://natehagens.substack.com/p/essay-how-to-think-about-the-future-92e> ,
    <https://www.thegreatsimplification.com/frankly-original/139-how-to-think-about-the-future-part-2> ;
    <http://example.org/thinkr#hasDiscussionSummary> "A genuinely foundational episode for this graph's own scenario-matrix structure, not just another Frankly. Nate opens by directly naming a cognitive trap — settling on one preferred future and then judging everything else against it — and argues for holding a real distribution of possible futures instead. The four-grid structure is built up methodically and explicitly incompletely: Nate states outright that technology and demographics were both considered for their own separate grids and deliberately excluded, arguing both function as amplifiers of whatever direction the underlying system is already moving rather than independent axes — AI specifically is framed as already inside the Earth Systems grid, since data-center electricity demand is itself a direct new draw on the energy/biophysical system the whole framework is built around. A real, distinctive moment of self-aware epistemic humility: Nate explicitly invites the audience to propose grids he hasn't considered, naming mental/physical health and freedom of information/communication as real candidates he chose not to include. The Geopolitical grid's own real-time grounding is notable — recorded during the actual 2026 Iran war and Strait of Hormuz closure, Nate uses that live, unfolding event as a direct real-world example of the 'adversarial and interdependent' quadrant, citing the Philippines' energy emergency, Bangladesh's school closures, and Japan's emergency oil-reserve release as concurrent real evidence. Nate draws a real, pointed distinction between what a contraction-vs-growth headline means for the 'fairness' question (during growth) versus the 'is there enough' question (during contraction) — the same quadrant axes, he argues, describe a fundamentally different lived experience depending on which economic direction they're paired with. The episode closes by explicitly refusing to let any single quadrant stand as a full future on its own — Nate states directly that nobody will 'live in The Great Simplification in general,' only in a Great Simplification combined with a specific power structure, geopolitical backdrop, and ecological condition, previewing Part 3's work building concrete scenario composites from these four layers."@en ;
    <http://example.org/thinkr#seriesPosition> 2 .

