<http://example.org/tgs#Monologue.Frankly_154_UnordinaryInterventions> a owl:NamedIndividual ;
    <http://example.org/thinkr#discusses> <http://example.org/tgs#Concept.CarbonPulse> ,
    <http://example.org/tgs#Concept.DistributedAgency> ,
    <http://example.org/tgs#Concept.Superorganism> ;
    <http://example.org/thinkr#hasCanonicalSource> <https://www.thegreatsimplification.com/frankly-original/154-unordinary-interventions> ;
    <http://example.org/thinkr#hasEpisodeSegment> <http://example.org/tgs#EpisodeSegment.Frankly> ;
    <http://example.org/thinkr#hasEpisodeType> <http://example.org/thinkr#EpisodeType.Monologue> ;
    <http://example.org/thinkr#hasHost> <http://example.org/tgs#Persona.NateHagens> ;
    <http://example.org/thinkr#hasReplay> <https://youtu.be/hMb9uBz-31A> ;
    <http://example.org/thinkr#hasTranscript> <https://www.thegreatsimplification.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/08/Frankly-154-Transcript-Unordinary-Interventions.pdf> ;
    <http://purl.org/dc/terms/creator> <http://example.org/tgs#Persona.NateHagens> ;
    <http://purl.org/dc/terms/source> <https://www.thegreatsimplification.com/frankly-original/154-unordinary-interventions> ;
    <http://example.org/thinkr#hasDiscussionSummary> "Opens with an unusually explicit methodological distinction Nate draws for himself as much as the audience: most 'what can I do' lists blur together self-protection (growing food, improving sleep, building a support network) with actually changing the trajectory of the metacrisis itself, and he states directly that everything offered here is aimed at the latter, not the former — 'nothing I offer here is primarily intended as just a coping skill.' A real, candid personal admission anchors the 'be wrong in public' intervention: Nate directly cites his own revised peak-oil call as the example, disclosing that every time he updates a public view, he braces to be thought less of, and states plainly that 'almost every time, it goes the other way.' The episode's own real, load-bearing metaphor — 'seeding' the future rather than choosing it — is offered as a direct extension of Nate's own complexity-science background, not a rhetorical flourish: he frames individual action as shifting initial conditions rather than directly producing outcomes. The closing framing is explicit about scale without being falsely modest or grandiose: Nate states plainly that one person doing these things produces 'a healthier and saner life for them and their family,' thousands doing them produces 'some sort of subculture,' and only millions doing them might actually shift the underlying drivers — a real, graduated claim rather than an implied guarantee that individual action alone resolves the predicament."@en .

<http://example.org/tgs#Monologue.Frankly_23_MordorEconomy> a owl:NamedIndividual ;
    <http://example.org/thinkr#hasCanonicalSource> <https://www.thegreatsimplification.com/frankly-original/23-mordor-economy> ;
    <http://example.org/thinkr#hasEpisodeSegment> <http://example.org/tgs#EpisodeSegment.Frankly> ;
    <http://example.org/thinkr#hasEpisodeType> <http://example.org/thinkr#EpisodeType.Monologue> ;
    <http://example.org/thinkr#hasHost> <http://example.org/tgs#Persona.NateHagens> ;
    <http://purl.org/dc/terms/creator> <http://example.org/tgs#Persona.NateHagens> ;
    <http://purl.org/dc/terms/source> <https://www.thegreatsimplification.com/frankly-original/23-mordor-economy> .

<http://example.org/tgs#Monologue.Frankly_47_FranklyReFranklys> a owl:NamedIndividual ;
    <http://example.org/thinkr#hasCanonicalSource> <https://www.thegreatsimplification.com/frankly-original/47-frankly-re-franklys> ;
    <http://example.org/thinkr#hasEpisodeType> <http://example.org/thinkr#EpisodeType.Monologue> ;
    <http://example.org/thinkr#hasHost> <http://example.org/tgs#Persona.NateHagens> ;
    <http://example.org/thinkr#hasTranscript> <https://youtu.be/ZJbKVu7vbLk> ;
    <http://purl.org/dc/terms/creator> <http://example.org/tgs#Persona.NateHagens> ;
    <http://purl.org/dc/terms/source> <https://www.thegreatsimplification.com/frankly-original/47-frankly-re-franklys> ;
    <http://example.org/thinkr#hasDiscussionSummary> "Genuinely raw and unscripted — Nate explicitly frames it as having 'no outline here, this is just a complete off-the-cuff riff,' and the delivery matches: real-time analysis of the Israel-Gaza war's likely trajectory and US debt/interest-rate dynamics, offered candidly as non-expert commentary rather than polished analysis ('I'm not an expert on that other than to say...'). The episode's real center of gravity is Nate directly stating the Frankly format's own purpose in his own words for the first time this explicitly: building awareness in a 'scout team' of engaged listeners, and secondarily attempting to shift the 'consciousness of those in power' who hold outsized leverage over institutional choices. A genuinely personal, vulnerable thread runs through the closing third: Nate discloses working with a coach on shifting from 'productivity' to 'awareness,' describes his own imperfect attempt at tech-free 'Silent Saturdays' ('doing kind of half-assed... because I'm so busy'), and states plainly, without hedging, 'I will never be anyone's guru... I'm not only an analyst but I'm a client' — real self-disclosure about his own use of the same practices he recommends, offered as an admission of imperfection rather than authority."@en .

<http://example.org/tgs#Monologue.Frankly_58_FellowshipBendNotBreak> a owl:NamedIndividual ;
    <http://example.org/thinkr#discusses> <http://example.org/tgs#Concept.CarbonPulse> ,
    <http://example.org/tgs#Concept.DistributedAgency> ;
    <http://example.org/thinkr#hasCanonicalSource> <https://www.thegreatsimplification.com/frankly-original/58-the-fellowship-of-the-ring-bend-not-break-version> ;
    <http://example.org/thinkr#hasEpisodeSegment> <http://example.org/tgs#EpisodeSegment.Frankly> ;
    <http://example.org/thinkr#hasEpisodeType> <http://example.org/thinkr#EpisodeType.Monologue> ;
    <http://example.org/thinkr#hasHost> <http://example.org/tgs#Persona.NateHagens> ;
    <http://example.org/thinkr#hasReplay> <https://youtu.be/3oh-zdo-l8I> ;
    <http://example.org/thinkr#hasTranscript> <https://www.thegreatsimplification.com/wp-content/uploads/2024/03/Frankly58Transcript.pdf> ;
    <http://purl.org/dc/terms/creator> <http://example.org/tgs#Persona.NateHagens> ;
    <http://purl.org/dc/terms/source> <https://www.thegreatsimplification.com/frankly-original/58-the-fellowship-of-the-ring-bend-not-break-version> ;
    <http://example.org/thinkr#hasDiscussionSummary> "A genuinely different register from most Franklys built this session — explicitly playful ('I loved the magic swords and the dragons') rather than analytical, though building toward a real, substantive point. Nate opens with an unusual, direct meta-apology for a prior episode's audio experiment ('I will never do that again'), then spends the bulk of the episode building a structured, 3-tier archetype system rather than a single flat list. The most intellectually interesting move is the direct 'morphs into' pairing for each business-as-usual archetype under contraction — not random decline, but a real, specific transformation Nate assigns to each (Banker to Hoarder via loss aversion specifically, Technologist to Cyborg via AI/big data specifically, Elite to a genuine fork he names directly: either a Hoarder building bunkers, or a real collective-response contributor). The episode's actual emotional core, by Nate's own explicit statement, isn't the archetype taxonomy itself but the feeling of 'camaraderie and fellowship' he associates with the source material — he states this directly as 'the most important part of this little Frankly reflection.' The episode closes on a real, generous reframe of individual agency: rather than requiring a Frodo-style singular hero, Nate points to Sam and Gollum as the real Catalysts who changed Lord of the Rings' outcome, offering this directly to listeners as a model for how an ordinary, unheroic role ('you do your job, live with your family, enjoy your life') can still meaningfully shift a larger outcome."@en .

<http://example.org/tgs#Monologue.Frankly_80_ThankYouForRuiningMyLife> a owl:NamedIndividual ;
    <http://example.org/thinkr#discusses> <http://example.org/tgs#Concept.DistributedAgency> ,
    <http://example.org/tgs#Concept.Superorganism> ;
    <http://example.org/thinkr#hasCanonicalSource> <https://www.thegreatsimplification.com/frankly-original/80-thank-you-for-ruining-my-life> ;
    <http://example.org/thinkr#hasEpisodeSegment> <http://example.org/tgs#EpisodeSegment.Frankly> ;
    <http://example.org/thinkr#hasEpisodeType> <http://example.org/thinkr#EpisodeType.Monologue> ;
    <http://example.org/thinkr#hasHost> <http://example.org/tgs#Persona.NateHagens> ;
    <http://example.org/thinkr#hasReplay> <https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=9wXwdcXJRzs> ;
    <http://purl.org/dc/terms/creator> <http://example.org/tgs#Persona.NateHagens> ;
    <http://purl.org/dc/terms/source> <https://www.thegreatsimplification.com/frankly-original/80-thank-you-for-ruining-my-life> ;
    <http://example.org/thinkr#hasDiscussionSummary> "Genuinely unscripted and personal, by Nate's own explicit description — 'these Frankly are just I have no notes at all other than the email I just read.' The episode's real emotional center is the listener's own words, read aloud: a self-described 'wrestling match' between techno-optimism and the podcast's systems synthesis, with each new layer of complexity (additional episodes, then outside books) initially adding discomfort and sleepless nights but ultimately making the overall picture more coherent rather than less. A real, candid personal admission from Nate follows directly: being recognized and engaged with substantively by a stranger 'in real life' lands differently and more powerfully than working alone in an office, even though he 'care[s] deeply about these issues' regardless. The episode's most quotable real formulation is Nate's own closing personal ethos — 'B+ in service of life' — offered as his real, current email signature and a direct acknowledgment that his own production quality is intentionally imperfect ('these could be way better produced... I'm just doing the best I can every week'). Nate is explicit and unambiguous that this openness isn't universal outreach: he directly states the podcast 'is not for everyone because it's intense, it's scary, there are no easy answers,' aimed specifically at people with the maturity to sit with discomfort rather than a broad audience. The episode closes on real, concrete forward-looking previews of Nate's own upcoming work, including an early, evolving version of ideas that would later inform the Frankly_132 six-fronts framework, and a real, direct tease of the following week's Frankly as a time-travel-themed piece connected to the Superorganism concept."@en .

