<http://example.org/tgs#PanelDiscussion.RR_10_TheEcologyOfCommunication> a owl:NamedIndividual ;
    <http://example.org/thinkr#discusses> <http://example.org/tgs#Concept.Metacrisis> ,
    <http://example.org/tgs#Concept.Schismogenesis> ,
    <http://example.org/tgs#SchoolOfThought.WarmData> ;
    <http://example.org/thinkr#hasCanonicalSource> <https://www.thegreatsimplification.com/episode/reality-roundtable-10> ;
    <http://example.org/thinkr#hasEpisodeSegment> <http://example.org/tgs#EpisodeSegment.RealityRoundtable> ;
    <http://example.org/thinkr#hasEpisodeType> <http://example.org/thinkr#EpisodeType.PanelDiscussion> ;
    <http://example.org/thinkr#hasGuest> <http://example.org/tgs#Persona.DanielSchmachtenberger> ,
    <http://example.org/tgs#Persona.NoraBateson> ,
    <http://example.org/tgs#Persona.RexWeyler> ,
    <http://example.org/tgs#Persona.VanessaAndreotti> ;
    <http://example.org/thinkr#hasHost> <http://example.org/tgs#Persona.NateHagens> ;
    <http://purl.org/dc/terms/contributor> <http://example.org/tgs#Persona.DanielSchmachtenberger> ,
    <http://example.org/tgs#Persona.NoraBateson> ,
    <http://example.org/tgs#Persona.RexWeyler> ,
    <http://example.org/tgs#Persona.VanessaAndreotti> ;
    <http://purl.org/dc/terms/creator> <http://example.org/tgs#Persona.NateHagens> ;
    <http://purl.org/dc/terms/source> <https://www.thegreatsimplification.com/episode/reality-roundtable-10> ;
    <http://example.org/thinkr#hasDiscussionSummary> "The most philosophically dense and least topic-driven of the built RRs — four guests who know each other's work well (Nate's own framing: 'you have been on my podcast, many of you more than once') build directly on each other rather than staying in their own lanes, so the discussion reads more like one extended, co-authored argument than four separate positions. Nora opens with the central frame (communication itself as ecology, not transmission); Rex immediately extends it with a direct ecological parallel (a monoculture forest versus a real one); Vanessa reframes it again through a decolonial lens (the difference between 'environment' as something external and 'ecology' as something we're inside of, and how English itself can fail to carry non-Western concepts of land); Daniel closes the opening round by grounding it further in physics and boundary theory (there are no truly separable things, only permeable exchange). None of the four ever pushes back on another's framing — disagreement essentially doesn't happen — but there is real, substantive addition happening in each turn, not just restatement. The one moment of process friction is instructive rather than adversarial: Daniel is candid that dialectical and empathetic communication tools can both be genuinely healing AND weaponized (a fact he treats as unresolved and requiring ongoing discernment, not a settled question), and he shares his own experience running couples counseling as the clearest illustration of how two people's honest accounts of the same relationship can diverge completely. Nate's own contribution is again unusually personal — he opens by admitting he was 'in a foul mood' before the conversation started and closes by directly stating his mood had genuinely lifted by the end, treating his own emotional shift as real evidence for the argument being made rather than an aside."@en .

<http://example.org/tgs#PanelDiscussion.RR_11_TalesFromTheCarbonPulse> a owl:NamedIndividual ;
    <http://example.org/thinkr#discusses> <http://example.org/tgs#Concept.CarbonPulse> ,
    <http://example.org/tgs#Concept.Superorganism> ;
    <http://example.org/thinkr#hasCanonicalSource> <https://www.thegreatsimplification.com/episode/reality-roundtable-11> ;
    <http://example.org/thinkr#hasEpisodeSegment> <http://example.org/tgs#EpisodeSegment.RealityRoundtable> ;
    <http://example.org/thinkr#hasEpisodeType> <http://example.org/thinkr#EpisodeType.PanelDiscussion> ;
    <http://example.org/thinkr#hasGuest> <http://example.org/tgs#Persona.DJWhite> ,
    <http://example.org/tgs#Persona.TomMurphy> ;
    <http://example.org/thinkr#hasHost> <http://example.org/tgs#Persona.NateHagens> ;
    <http://example.org/thinkr#hasTranscript> <https://www.thegreatsimplification.com/wp-content/uploads/2024/10/RR11TranscriptTalesfromtheCarbonPulse.pdf> ;
    <http://purl.org/dc/terms/contributor> <http://example.org/tgs#Persona.DJWhite> ,
    <http://example.org/tgs#Persona.TomMurphy> ;
    <http://purl.org/dc/terms/creator> <http://example.org/tgs#Persona.NateHagens> ;
    <http://purl.org/dc/terms/source> <https://www.thegreatsimplification.com/episode/reality-roundtable-11> ;
    <http://example.org/thinkr#hasDiscussionSummary> "An especially close, long-standing friendship — all three explicitly reference exchanging 'thousands of emails over the last 10 years' — but not one that suppresses real disagreement. The clearest substantive divergence: Tom Murphy proposes, newly and tentatively, that collapsing global fertility rates could drive 'peak civilizational power' as early as 2033 through demand-side contraction rather than supply-side resource limits — Nate directly pushes back ('I'm going to push back on that... I think that's going to be a much bigger constraint... than the demographics'), and DJ White, while finding Murphy's data 'a head spinner,' states plainly he still comes down on the side of supply constraints. None of the three resolve this into agreement; it's held open. A real, notable moment of live intellectual honesty: Murphy explicitly describes changing his own mind mid-conversation, calling the demographic argument something he'd 'never considered before' and being 'gobsmacked' by the fertility data. DJ White's own account of he and his wife's decision not to have more biological children — framed provocatively as 'DNA is essentially our enemy at this point' — is offered candidly as central to his own philosophy, not softened. White also introduces a real, distinctive naming device mid-conversation: naming the hypothetical trillionth human being 'Naya' as a rhetorical anchor for thinking in deep time about population ethics. Murphy's own disillusionment with academia is disclosed personally and specifically — recounting 'blank stares' from colleagues he considered geniuses when he tried to discuss civilizational limits, and citing this disappointment as a real factor in his decision to retire at 53. The recurring 'man rushing at you with a hammer' analogy, explicitly credited to Dennis Meadows, becomes the episode's central reframe of the renewable-energy debate — arguing the relevant question isn't which weapon (fossil vs. renewable) but why there's an attacker at all."@en .

<http://example.org/tgs#PanelDiscussion.RR_12_TheGlobalStakesOfTheUSElection> a owl:NamedIndividual ;
    <http://example.org/thinkr#hasCanonicalSource> <https://www.thegreatsimplification.com/episode/reality-roundtable-12> ;
    <http://example.org/thinkr#hasEpisodeSegment> <http://example.org/tgs#EpisodeSegment.RealityRoundtable> ;
    <http://example.org/thinkr#hasEpisodeType> <http://example.org/thinkr#EpisodeType.PanelDiscussion> ;
    <http://example.org/thinkr#hasGuest> <http://example.org/tgs#Persona.LukeGromen> ,
    <http://example.org/tgs#Persona.MichaelEvery> ;
    <http://example.org/thinkr#hasHost> <http://example.org/tgs#Persona.NateHagens> ;
    <http://example.org/thinkr#hasTranscript> <https://www.thegreatsimplification.com/wp-content/uploads/2024/10/RR12TranscriptEveryGromen.docx.pdf> ;
    <http://purl.org/dc/terms/contributor> <http://example.org/tgs#Persona.LukeGromen> ,
    <http://example.org/tgs#Persona.MichaelEvery> ;
    <http://purl.org/dc/terms/creator> <http://example.org/tgs#Persona.NateHagens> ;
    <http://purl.org/dc/terms/source> <https://www.thegreatsimplification.com/episode/reality-roundtable-12> ;
    <http://example.org/thinkr#hasDiscussionSummary> "Both guests name their own dynamic explicitly at the outset — 'we agree on about 90 percent... where we disagree is in what we think some of the short term, medium term and long term outcomes might be' — and the conversation lives up to that self-description: substantive, technical, real-time disagreement between two people who like and respect each other, not a debate performance. The central, sustained divergence is Gromen's case for gold re-emerging as a neutral reserve asset tied to energy value (his own vivid example: a wife's 8,000-pound truck becoming uneconomical once gasoline has to be paid for in finite, appreciating gold) against Every's case that fiat survives via targeted, directed credit and capital controls rather than a hard-money return — neither concedes the point, and Every explicitly names the shape of their disagreement himself near the close: 'it's like a slightly Rorschach... you're seeing one thing in it, I'm seeing something similar, but a bit different.' The one genuinely unexpected personal disclosure is Every's own account of being raised in a Marxist household in Europe, offered candidly as real biographical context for his read on China, not as a rhetorical move. A recurring shared device — the 'WWJD' bracelet, tied back to economist Joseph Schumpeter's own late-life religious turn — becomes their common ground for arguing that no monetary system, gold-backed or fiat, works without an underlying shared sense of what the economy is actually for. Nate's own framing of the roundtable format itself as a real Overton-window mechanism is worth noting directly: he suggests ideas normalized in conversations like this one may filter into real policy discourse, a claim Every's own professional paper (independently confirmed as hosted on TGS's own site) lends some real weight to."@en .

<http://example.org/tgs#PanelDiscussion.RR_13_FutureCouncil> a owl:NamedIndividual ;
    <http://example.org/thinkr#discusses> <http://example.org/tgs#Concept.Superorganism> ;
    <http://example.org/thinkr#hasCanonicalSource> <https://www.thegreatsimplification.com/episode/reality-roundtable-13> ;
    <http://example.org/thinkr#hasEpisodeSegment> <http://example.org/tgs#EpisodeSegment.RealityRoundtable> ;
    <http://example.org/thinkr#hasEpisodeType> <http://example.org/thinkr#EpisodeType.PanelDiscussion> ;
    <http://example.org/thinkr#hasGuest> <http://example.org/tgs#Persona.ClemenceCurrie> ,
    <http://example.org/tgs#Persona.DamonGameau> ,
    <http://example.org/tgs#Persona.JoaquinMinana> ,
    <http://example.org/tgs#Persona.SkyeNeville> ;
    <http://example.org/thinkr#hasHost> <http://example.org/tgs#Persona.NateHagens> ;
    <http://example.org/thinkr#hasTranscript> <https://youtu.be/gi5VXVc2N6c> ;
    <http://purl.org/dc/terms/contributor> <http://example.org/tgs#Persona.ClemenceCurrie> ,
    <http://example.org/tgs#Persona.DamonGameau> ,
    <http://example.org/tgs#Persona.JoaquinMinana> ,
    <http://example.org/tgs#Persona.SkyeNeville> ;
    <http://purl.org/dc/terms/creator> <http://example.org/tgs#Persona.NateHagens> ;
    <http://purl.org/dc/terms/source> <https://www.thegreatsimplification.com/episode/reality-roundtable-13> ;
    <http://example.org/thinkr#hasDiscussionSummary> "A genuinely distinct episode in tone and format — Nate first interviews adult director Damon Gameau briefly before three young activists join. Gameau's own central intellectual contribution is his 'Three Hats' framework: that people (including the corporate leaders he filmed) are simultaneously economic actors, individuals seeking a good life, and aspiring good ancestors, and that current economic structures make it nearly impossible to hold all three at once — a real, substantive explanation for why even caring executives feel trapped. He discloses candidly that the filmmaking process personally 'cracked him open' emotionally, his most profound experience as a director. The three young guests offer real, differentiated (not uniform) assessments of their actual corporate meetings: they describe Decathlon's response as genuinely responsive (one of their suggestions — multiple name-tag spaces on clothing for reuse — was reportedly put into practice), in direct contrast to Nestlé and ING, which they describe as evasive when pressed on fossil-fuel-to-renewable transition plans. Their own economic reasoning is notably sophisticated for their age: a direct 'drug dealer' analogy (framing Exxon and Shell as suppliers who shift blame onto addicted consumers) and a separate 'cookie' analogy illustrating how blame gets redirected from the powerful onto the vulnerable. Skye Neville discloses a real, unresolved personal tension rather than claiming false consistency: extensive international travel for the film's promotion directly conflicts with her own environmental values, which she addresses through concrete but self-acknowledged imperfect offsetting (50 consecutive beach cleanups, tree planting) rather than denial of the contradiction. Nate's own closing philosophical framing — describing modern flight as 'indistinguishable from magic,' powered by dense ancient energy most people take entirely for granted — is offered as a direct extension of the conversation rather than a scripted transition. The episode closes with practical, peer-to-peer advice from the children themselves on managing climate anxiety: taking small concrete actions, allowing themselves to grieve openly rather than suppressing distress, and resisting the pressure to personally solve the crisis alone."@en .

<http://example.org/tgs#PanelDiscussion.RR_14_TheFutureIsLocal> a owl:NamedIndividual ;
    <http://example.org/thinkr#discusses> <http://example.org/tgs#Concept.Superorganism> ;
    <http://example.org/thinkr#hasCanonicalSource> <https://www.thegreatsimplification.com/episode/reality-roundtable-14> ;
    <http://example.org/thinkr#hasEpisodeSegment> <http://example.org/tgs#EpisodeSegment.RealityRoundtable> ;
    <http://example.org/thinkr#hasEpisodeType> <http://example.org/thinkr#EpisodeType.PanelDiscussion> ;
    <http://example.org/thinkr#hasGuest> <http://example.org/tgs#Persona.DanielChristianWahl> ,
    <http://example.org/tgs#Persona.IsabelCarlisle> ,
    <http://example.org/tgs#Persona.SamanthaPower> ;
    <http://example.org/thinkr#hasHost> <http://example.org/tgs#Persona.NateHagens> ;
    <http://example.org/thinkr#hasTranscript> <https://www.thegreatsimplification.com/wp-content/uploads/2025/01/RR14Bioregioning101Transcript.pdf> ;
    <http://purl.org/dc/terms/contributor> <http://example.org/tgs#Persona.DanielChristianWahl> ,
    <http://example.org/tgs#Persona.IsabelCarlisle> ,
    <http://example.org/tgs#Persona.SamanthaPower> ;
    <http://purl.org/dc/terms/creator> <http://example.org/tgs#Persona.NateHagens> ;
    <http://purl.org/dc/terms/source> <https://www.thegreatsimplification.com/episode/reality-roundtable-14> ;
    <http://example.org/thinkr#hasDiscussionSummary> "Framed explicitly by Nate as '101' for himself as much as the audience — he opens by admitting he 'knew next to nothing on bioregioning' before this recording, and the episode maintains that genuinely introductory, low-friction register throughout, with Wahl, Carlisle, and Power building one shared framework together rather than debating. The three guests' expertise divides cleanly and complementarily: Carlisle grounds the conversation in place and deep history (including her own vivid personal anecdote from her earlier archaeology career, recovering a woolly mammoth tusk at a 250,000-year-old Neanderthal site in Jersey), Wahl supplies the philosophical and historical framing (regenerative design, indigenous land-management history, a direct real citation of John Wesley Powell's 1879 proposal to organize the US by watershed boundaries), and Power supplies the financial and governance architecture via her own book's 'four returns' framework. The one real moment of internal critical challenge, rather than mutual reinforcement, comes from Wahl directly to Power: a pointed concern about whether bioregional finance risks becoming 'a backdoor for the finance industry to still be extractive' if patient capital is allowed to expect conventional financial returns — Power responds with a structured, real answer grounded in her book's own stated design principles (purpose-aligned funders, returns as a means rather than an end) rather than deflecting the concern. Wahl closes the episode by reciting a Gary Snyder poem in full in response to Nate's closing question about inevitability — the content of the poem itself is not reproduced here per this graph's own copyright discipline, only the fact of the recitation. The episode ends on a concrete, real organizing detail: plans are underway for a North American Bioregional Congress in 2026, the first since 2009."@en .

<http://example.org/tgs#PanelDiscussion.RR_15_ThePlasticCrisis> a owl:NamedIndividual ;
    <http://example.org/thinkr#discusses> <http://example.org/tgs#Concept.CarbonPulse> ;
    <http://example.org/thinkr#hasCanonicalSource> <https://www.thegreatsimplification.com/episode/reality-roundtable-15> ;
    <http://example.org/thinkr#hasEpisodeSegment> <http://example.org/tgs#EpisodeSegment.RealityRoundtable> ;
    <http://example.org/thinkr#hasEpisodeType> <http://example.org/thinkr#EpisodeType.PanelDiscussion> ;
    <http://example.org/thinkr#hasGuest> <http://example.org/tgs#Persona.ChristinaDixon> ,
    <http://example.org/tgs#Persona.LeoTrasande> ,
    <http://example.org/tgs#Persona.LindaBirnbaum> ;
    <http://example.org/thinkr#hasHost> <http://example.org/tgs#Persona.NateHagens> ;
    <http://example.org/thinkr#hasTranscript> <https://www.thegreatsimplification.com/wp-content/uploads/2025/02/RR15TranscriptPlasticCrisis.pdf> ;
    <http://purl.org/dc/terms/contributor> <http://example.org/tgs#Persona.ChristinaDixon> ,
    <http://example.org/tgs#Persona.LeoTrasande> ,
    <http://example.org/tgs#Persona.LindaBirnbaum> ;
    <http://purl.org/dc/terms/creator> <http://example.org/tgs#Persona.NateHagens> ;
    <http://purl.org/dc/terms/source> <https://www.thegreatsimplification.com/episode/reality-roundtable-15> ;
    <http://example.org/thinkr#hasDiscussionSummary> "Deeply collegial and values-aligned throughout — Nate himself names the real dissonance directly: 'off camera... we were laughing and joking... and it's fricking disgusting.' The episode opens with a real intellectual move worth distinguishing from simple agreement: asked whether they agree with Jeremy Grantham's claim (made in a separate real TGS episode) that plastics/EDCs pose a bigger threat than climate change, Birnbaum partially concurs, but Dixon and Trasande both immediately reject the framing itself — arguing plastics and climate are inseparable, not competing threats, since plastics are fossil fuels and their production directly undermines any 1.5°C pathway. That reframing, not the original yes/no question, becomes the episode's real throughline. Nate's own CPAP mask is used as a recurring, genuinely personal anchor for his anxiety about untestable everyday plastic exposure. The clearest moment of real, extended speculation is Trasande's direct comparison to The Handmaid's Tale, predicting future conflict not simply over food and water but specifically over safe, uncontaminated food and water — Nate responds candidly that he'd 'never thought of that before,' a real live reaction rather than a rehearsed follow-up. Dixon deliberately pivots the conversation's tone using the Montreal Protocol as a real model of policy success, naming the shift herself ('I felt like we did go a bit doom mongering there for a second'). The episode's most vivid first-hand account is Trasande's own description of being the last public speaker at the Busan plastics-treaty negotiations at 2:30am, with Dixon revealing she stayed in the room specifically to support him. Nate's closing reframe of Dixon's self-deprecating 'I wish I was rich' comment — 'you are already rich, given what you're doing with your life' — is a small, values-driven correction offered warmly rather than argued."@en .

<http://example.org/tgs#PanelDiscussion.RR_16_TheMadScrambleForPower> a owl:NamedIndividual ;
    <http://example.org/thinkr#hasCanonicalSource> <https://www.thegreatsimplification.com/episode/reality-roundtable-16> ;
    <http://example.org/thinkr#hasEpisodeSegment> <http://example.org/tgs#EpisodeSegment.RealityRoundtable> ;
    <http://example.org/thinkr#hasEpisodeType> <http://example.org/thinkr#EpisodeType.PanelDiscussion> ;
    <http://example.org/thinkr#hasGuest> <http://example.org/tgs#Persona.ArtBerman> ,
    <http://example.org/tgs#Persona.IzabellaKaminska> ,
    <http://example.org/tgs#Persona.MichaelEvery> ;
    <http://example.org/thinkr#hasHost> <http://example.org/tgs#Persona.NateHagens> ;
    <http://example.org/thinkr#hasTranscript> <https://www.thegreatsimplification.com/wp-content/uploads/2025/04/RR16TranscriptTheMadScrambleforPower.pdf> ;
    <http://purl.org/dc/terms/contributor> <http://example.org/tgs#Persona.ArtBerman> ,
    <http://example.org/tgs#Persona.IzabellaKaminska> ,
    <http://example.org/tgs#Persona.MichaelEvery> ;
    <http://purl.org/dc/terms/creator> <http://example.org/tgs#Persona.NateHagens> ;
    <http://purl.org/dc/terms/source> <https://www.thegreatsimplification.com/episode/reality-roundtable-16> ;
    <http://example.org/thinkr#hasDiscussionSummary> "A sophisticated, largely convergent three-way analysis among established macro thinkers, organized around Michael Every's own Biblical framing — a civilizational shift from 'childlike' to 'adult' understanding of energy's centrality — which Kaminska and Berman both independently extend rather than contest ('geology is not fair,' Every's line, gets Berman's direct addition: 'geography isn't fair' either). The clearest moment of real, if gentle, divergence is Berman calling the fear of a Russian invasion of Europe 'a preposterous notion' — Every doesn't rebut this directly but offers a genuinely different interpretive frame, invoking Leo Strauss's theory that societies need a unifying 'noble lie' or bogeyman to function, leaving Nate visibly updating live ('I'd never thought about it that way'). Kaminska's own original conceptual contribution — 'mutual liberation syndrome,' her term for how each side of a polarized society sees itself as the one fighting authoritarianism — is offered as a real, named framework, not just commentary. Every's own account of learning 'face culture' through his in-laws (a vivid, personal anecdote about blame being physically and socially transferred to a child after his mother-in-law's flatulence) grounds an otherwise abstract geopolitical point about states' unwillingness to admit error. The episode's most sustained extended metaphor is Every's own, built around Battlestar Galactica (both the 1970s original and its 2000s reboot) and later The Wire and Top Boy, arguing that policymakers who have never experienced real zero-sum competition are now playing exactly that game at the highest levels without understanding its rules. All three converge, without qualification, on honesty from leadership as the single highest-value intervention available — though each frames the mechanism differently: Berman via small 'islands of coherence' of genuinely informed people, Kaminska via leaders directly admitting past policy failures, Every via urging policymakers to study realistic depictions of zero-sum power rather than idealized ones."@en .

<http://example.org/tgs#PanelDiscussion.RR_17_MovingFromApathyToAction> a owl:NamedIndividual ;
    <http://example.org/thinkr#discusses> <http://example.org/tgs#Concept.DistributedAgency> ;
    <http://example.org/thinkr#hasCanonicalSource> <https://www.thegreatsimplification.com/episode/reality-roundtable-17> ;
    <http://example.org/thinkr#hasEpisodeSegment> <http://example.org/tgs#EpisodeSegment.RealityRoundtable> ;
    <http://example.org/thinkr#hasEpisodeType> <http://example.org/thinkr#EpisodeType.PanelDiscussion> ;
    <http://example.org/thinkr#hasGuest> <http://example.org/tgs#Persona.JohnSeed> ,
    <http://example.org/tgs#Persona.SkyeCielitaFlor> ;
    <http://example.org/thinkr#hasHost> <http://example.org/tgs#Persona.NateHagens> ;
    <http://example.org/thinkr#hasTranscript> <https://www.thegreatsimplification.com/wp-content/uploads/2025/07/RR-17-Transcript-Moving-from-Apathy-to-Action.pdf> ;
    <http://purl.org/dc/terms/contributor> <http://example.org/tgs#Persona.JohnSeed> ,
    <http://example.org/tgs#Persona.SkyeCielitaFlor> ;
    <http://purl.org/dc/terms/creator> <http://example.org/tgs#Persona.NateHagens> ;
    <http://purl.org/dc/terms/source> <https://www.thegreatsimplification.com/episode/reality-roundtable-17> ;
    <http://example.org/thinkr#hasDiscussionSummary> "Warm, spiritually direct, and unusually candid about Nate's own past skepticism — he opens by admitting that despite encountering Joanna Macy's work repeatedly between 2005 and 2020, 'it never resonated with me' until recently, a genuine disclosure of a changed position rather than a rhetorical setup. Seed and Flor hold a real teacher-student relationship (Flor explicitly introduces herself as 'a student of John'), but the conversation is not simply hierarchical — Flor develops her own distinct specialization (what she calls 'animist perception,' grounded in Stephen Harrod Buhner's plant-intelligence framework) that Seed openly defers to as outside his own primary focus. Seed's central intellectual contribution is a real evolutionary-biology argument that feelings predate and are more reliable than cognition, having been selected for across nearly all of evolutionary history, while cognition is 'a recent intruder.' Both guests offer real, vivid personal origin stories: Seed's account of a spontaneous 1979 direct action to protect what became Terania Creek rainforest, which he describes feeling like 'a mental breakdown' in the moment; Flor's account of witnessing an oil spill devastate an indigenous fishing community she was living with in the Peruvian Amazon, which directly redirected her work. A real moment of practical honesty rather than idealism: Seed recounts being asked to rebrand the Council of All Beings as corporate 'team building,' and reports it has failed every single time he tried — the ritual depends on authentic shared intention, not technique alone. Seed also discloses a genuinely humbling realization: after co-creating the Council of All Beings with Joanna Macy in 1985, he later witnessed Hopi practitioners performing what he recognized as the same ritual, which they told him they'd been doing for 10,000 years without interruption — a real moment of discovering rediscovery rather than invention. The episode closes on full, unqualified convergence between both guests on the same practical advice (find like-minded people close to home), which Nate notes directly echoes something said, coincidentally, on a completely unrelated podcast he recorded the same day."@en .

<http://example.org/tgs#PanelDiscussion.RR_18_HowWaterShapesOurPlanet> a owl:NamedIndividual ;
    <http://example.org/thinkr#hasCanonicalSource> <https://www.thegreatsimplification.com/episode/reality-roundtable-18> ;
    <http://example.org/thinkr#hasEpisodeSegment> <http://example.org/tgs#EpisodeSegment.RealityRoundtable> ;
    <http://example.org/thinkr#hasEpisodeType> <http://example.org/thinkr#EpisodeType.PanelDiscussion> ;
    <http://example.org/thinkr#hasGuest> <http://example.org/tgs#Persona.HeatherCooley> ,
    <http://example.org/tgs#Persona.MikeJoy> ,
    <http://example.org/tgs#Persona.ZachWeiss> ;
    <http://example.org/thinkr#hasHost> <http://example.org/tgs#Persona.NateHagens> ;
    <http://example.org/thinkr#hasTranscript> <https://www.thegreatsimplification.com/wp-content/uploads/2025/09/RR-18-Transcript-How-Water-Shapes-Our-Planet.pdf> ;
    <http://purl.org/dc/terms/contributor> <http://example.org/tgs#Persona.HeatherCooley> ,
    <http://example.org/tgs#Persona.MikeJoy> ,
    <http://example.org/tgs#Persona.ZachWeiss> ;
    <http://purl.org/dc/terms/creator> <http://example.org/tgs#Persona.NateHagens> ;
    <http://purl.org/dc/terms/source> <https://www.thegreatsimplification.com/episode/reality-roundtable-18> ;
    <http://example.org/thinkr#hasDiscussionSummary> "Genuinely complementary across three distinct scales rather than competing framings: Cooley supplies US institutional/policy analysis (the water-energy nexus, federal underinvestment), Joy supplies pointed regulatory critique grounded in decades fighting New Zealand's own dairy-industry water pollution, and Weiss supplies hands-on, decentralized landscape restoration practice. Joy's own biography is genuinely unusual and disclosed candidly: he left school at 17, worked as a truck driver, sheep farmer, and New Zealand Security Intelligence Service agent before enrolling at university at 33 — a real, non-academic path into a career built on 'blunt commentary' that has drawn criticism from the dairy industry and a former Prime Minister by name. Weiss's own account of the 'biotic pump' — living ecosystems drawing atmospheric moisture inland from the oceans — includes a real moment of scientific humility rather than overclaiming: his own words describe '72 anomalies of water' that remain without full physiological or physical explanation. The clearest moment of real, pointed institutional criticism is Joy's account of New Zealand's practical non-enforcement of its own environmental rules, describing the effective 'penalty' for water-pollution noncompliance as negligible. Rather than converging on a single lever of change, all three hold their differing theories of intervention (policy reform, regulatory enforcement, decentralized restoration) as genuinely complementary rather than competing, closing on shared, concrete real-world success stories — sponge cities, spring restoration, and community-led decentralized water-retention projects — as evidence for cautious, grounded hope rather than abstract optimism."@en .

<http://example.org/tgs#PanelDiscussion.RR_19_DarkTriad> a owl:NamedIndividual ;
    <http://example.org/thinkr#discusses> <http://example.org/tgs#Concept.DarkTriad> ,
    <http://example.org/tgs#Concept.Superorganism> ;
    <http://example.org/thinkr#hasCanonicalSource> <https://www.thegreatsimplification.com/episode/reality-roundtable-19> ;
    <http://example.org/thinkr#hasEpisodeSegment> <http://example.org/tgs#EpisodeSegment.RealityRoundtable> ;
    <http://example.org/thinkr#hasEpisodeType> <http://example.org/thinkr#EpisodeType.PanelDiscussion> ;
    <http://example.org/thinkr#hasGuest> <http://example.org/tgs#Persona.NancyMcWilliams> ,
    <http://example.org/tgs#Persona.ReidMeloy> ;
    <http://example.org/thinkr#hasHost> <http://example.org/tgs#Persona.NateHagens> ;
    <http://example.org/thinkr#hasTranscript> <https://www.thegreatsimplification.com/wp-content/uploads/2025/09/RR-19-Transcript-Dark-Triad-Personality-Traits.pdf> ;
    <http://purl.org/dc/terms/contributor> <http://example.org/tgs#Persona.NancyMcWilliams> ,
    <http://example.org/tgs#Persona.ReidMeloy> ;
    <http://purl.org/dc/terms/creator> <http://example.org/tgs#Persona.NateHagens> ;
    <http://purl.org/dc/terms/source> <https://www.thegreatsimplification.com/episode/reality-roundtable-19> ;
    <http://example.org/thinkr#hasDiscussionSummary> "Complementary rather than adversarial, but distinct in texture from the other RRs — Nancy McWilliams and Reid Meloy defer to and build on each other throughout rather than debate, yet each brings a genuinely different disciplinary lens rather than illustrative texture around a shared thesis. McWilliams opens by explicitly distancing her own approach from trait-based diagnosis ('I don't tend to think in terms of traits, I tend to think in terms of themes'), then supplies detailed clinical material Meloy doesn't have — how she actually works with psychopathic patients in therapy, her critique of the DSM's own diagnostic history. Meloy supplies forensic and evolutionary material she doesn't have — population prevalence estimates, predator/prey framing, extreme case data from his own casework. The one moment of honest gap rather than synthesis: when Nate raises Scott Barry Kaufman's 'Light Triad,' Meloy admits he isn't familiar with the term at all, and McWilliams is the one who has looked it up and explains it. Where the two genuinely converge, they amplify rather than merely agree — when McWilliams calls the entire history of the United States 'somewhat psychopathic,' Meloy doesn't qualify it, he extends it ('the theme of psychopathy has been woven through the history of the United States'). Nate's own contribution goes beyond interviewing: he draws a real cross-disciplinary connection between his own energy/power background and the psychological concept of power ('power in an ecology sense is an ecosystem... accessing an energy gradient per unit time'), and at the close, builds Meloy's closing metaphor (Dark Triad individuals as 'passengers on the train' who must never be allowed to drive) directly into his own recurring Superorganism framing, explicitly flagging he needs to think further about how the two connect rather than presenting it as settled."@en .

<http://example.org/tgs#PanelDiscussion.RR_1_ElectricVehicles> a owl:NamedIndividual ;
    <http://example.org/thinkr#discusses> <http://example.org/tgs#Concept.EnergyBlindness> ,
    <http://example.org/tgs#Concept.EnergyReturnOnInvestment> ;
    <http://example.org/thinkr#hasCanonicalSource> <https://www.thegreatsimplification.com/episode/rr01-berman-michaux-prieto> ;
    <http://example.org/thinkr#hasEpisodeSegment> <http://example.org/tgs#EpisodeSegment.RealityRoundtable> ;
    <http://example.org/thinkr#hasEpisodeType> <http://example.org/thinkr#EpisodeType.PanelDiscussion> ;
    <http://example.org/thinkr#hasGuest> <http://example.org/tgs#Persona.ArtBerman> ,
    <http://example.org/tgs#Persona.PedroPrieto> ,
    <http://example.org/tgs#Persona.SimonMichaux> ;
    <http://example.org/thinkr#hasHost> <http://example.org/tgs#Persona.NateHagens> ;
    <http://example.org/thinkr#hasTranscript> <https://www.thegreatsimplification.com/wp-content/uploads/2023/06/RR01BermanMichauxPrietoTranscript.docx1_.pdf> ;
    <http://purl.org/dc/terms/contributor> <http://example.org/tgs#Persona.ArtBerman> ,
    <http://example.org/tgs#Persona.PedroPrieto> ,
    <http://example.org/tgs#Persona.SimonMichaux> ;
    <http://purl.org/dc/terms/creator> <http://example.org/tgs#Persona.NateHagens> ;
    <http://purl.org/dc/terms/source> <https://www.thegreatsimplification.com/episode/rr01-berman-michaux-prieto> ;
    <http://example.org/thinkr#hasDiscussionSummary> "Largely a consensus-building conversation rather than a debate — the three guests, longtime friends and repeat TGS guests with overlapping biophysical/systems worldviews, converge on a shared thesis rather than argue against each other. Michaux opens with a detailed materials analysis showing that scaling EVs and renewables to meet stated EU 2030 targets would require multiples of known global reserves and annual production of key metals, even before accounting for grid capacity, hydrogen production, or the fossil fuels still required to make steel, plastic, and concrete. Berman and Prieto largely corroborate and extend this from their own domains rather than push back — Berman explicitly says 'I agree with you, I provided you data to support your perspective,' and adds that oil companies' underinvestment is a financial-market choice, not a resource-scarcity problem; Prieto brings real Spanish/German grid-stress data and EV-affordability numbers. The closest thing to friction is clarifying, not adversarial — Berman and Prieto probe Michaux on his 28-day storage-buffer assumption and the electric/hydrogen vehicle split, and he candidly revises and defends specific figures under that questioning, at one point calling the underlying 'is this sensible?' question 'the best argument for degrowth.' The one moment of genuine, if mild, divergence comes in the closing 'how could you be wrong' round: Michaux allows an unforeseen technological breakthrough could upend his numbers; Prieto reframes his own uncertainty as being about timing rather than direction ('sooner than later the system will collapse'); and Berman draws a sharper line between his own specific technical claims (which he says could be wrong) and the biodiversity/ecosystem decline data underlying the whole discussion (which he considers not meaningfully disputable). All three land on the same practical conclusion Nate frames at the top: current EV scaling plans reflect 'energy blindness' — a narrow, supply-side technology story substituting for the harder question of reducing consumption."@en .

<http://example.org/tgs#PanelDiscussion.RR_20_HackingHumanAttachment> a owl:NamedIndividual ;
    <http://example.org/thinkr#discusses> <http://example.org/tgs#Concept.CarbonPulse> ,
    <http://example.org/tgs#Concept.DarkTriad> ,
    <http://example.org/tgs#Concept.Superorganism> ;
    <http://example.org/thinkr#hasCanonicalSource> <https://www.thegreatsimplification.com/episode/reality-roundtable-20> ;
    <http://example.org/thinkr#hasEpisodeSegment> <http://example.org/tgs#EpisodeSegment.RealityRoundtable> ;
    <http://example.org/thinkr#hasEpisodeType> <http://example.org/thinkr#EpisodeType.PanelDiscussion> ;
    <http://example.org/thinkr#hasGuest> <http://example.org/tgs#Persona.NoraBateson> ,
    <http://example.org/tgs#Persona.ZakStein> ;
    <http://example.org/thinkr#hasHost> <http://example.org/tgs#Persona.NateHagens> ;
    <http://example.org/thinkr#hasTranscript> <https://www.thegreatsimplification.com/wp-content/uploads/2025/11/RR-20-Transcript-Hacking-Human-Attachment-1.pdf> ;
    <http://purl.org/dc/terms/contributor> <http://example.org/tgs#Persona.NoraBateson> ,
    <http://example.org/tgs#Persona.ZakStein> ;
    <http://purl.org/dc/terms/creator> <http://example.org/tgs#Persona.NateHagens> ;
    <http://purl.org/dc/terms/source> <https://www.thegreatsimplification.com/episode/reality-roundtable-20> ;
    <http://example.org/thinkr#hasDiscussionSummary> "A real division of labor between two friends who never disagree but consistently work from different registers — Zak Stein supplies the mechanistic explanation (mirror neurons, the attachment system versus the attentional system, Harlow's monkeys and operant conditioning, the historical throughline from B.F. Skinner to AI tutors), while Nora Bateson consistently reframes the same point in relational/felt terms (warm data, the missing 'rhythm of breath' in digital communication, the edge of the self). Neither treats this as competing frameworks; each explicitly builds on the other's point rather than substituting for it. The one moment resembling internal friction is Zak checking his own argument's edges rather than disagreeing with Nora — catching himself mid-critique to note that dismissing all technology outright is its own kind of naivety ('how far will you push your techno-pessimism'), and that the same critical tools used against chatbots could equally be pointed at real technological benefits (Novocaine, reliable garbage collection) he doesn't actually want to lose. Nate's own participation is unusually personal for an interview host — he narrates his own emotional arc in real time (a 'pit in my stomach' partway through, open shame about once mocking the Amish for opting out of exactly this risk, then explicit relief and hope by the close), and at one point the conversation is genuinely interrupted by an unplanned real-world moment — a garbage truck's noise triggering his own threat-detection reflex — which he and the guests fold directly into the discussion rather than treat as a distraction, since it illustrates the very embodied, evolved cognition the conversation argues chatbots are bypassing. The clearest moment of real-time concept-building: Nate offers 'wide boundary self-awareness' as his own gloss on Nora's father's old definition of sanity ('the ability to perceive your own epistemology'), and Nora accepts and extends it rather than correcting it."@en .

<http://example.org/tgs#PanelDiscussion.RR_21_ArmsRaceOrTheHumanRace> a owl:NamedIndividual ;
    <http://example.org/thinkr#discusses> <http://example.org/tgs#Concept.DarkTriad> ;
    <http://example.org/thinkr#hasCanonicalSource> <https://www.thegreatsimplification.com/episode/reality-roundtable-21> ;
    <http://example.org/thinkr#hasEpisodeSegment> <http://example.org/tgs#EpisodeSegment.RealityRoundtable> ;
    <http://example.org/thinkr#hasEpisodeType> <http://example.org/thinkr#EpisodeType.PanelDiscussion> ;
    <http://example.org/thinkr#hasGuest> <http://example.org/tgs#Persona.ChuckWatson> ,
    <http://example.org/tgs#Persona.MarkMedish> ;
    <http://example.org/thinkr#hasHost> <http://example.org/tgs#Persona.NateHagens> ;
    <http://example.org/thinkr#hasTranscript> <https://www.thegreatsimplification.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/01/RR-21-Transcript-Why-Science-Communication-Fails.pdf> ;
    <http://purl.org/dc/terms/contributor> <http://example.org/tgs#Persona.ChuckWatson> ,
    <http://example.org/tgs#Persona.MarkMedish> ;
    <http://purl.org/dc/terms/creator> <http://example.org/tgs#Persona.NateHagens> ;
    <http://purl.org/dc/terms/source> <https://www.thegreatsimplification.com/episode/reality-roundtable-21> ;
    <http://example.org/thinkr#hasDiscussionSummary> "Unlike several of the more adversarial-leaning built RRs, Medish and Watson are remarkably aligned throughout — this reads as two experts building one converging analysis together rather than staying in separate lanes or challenging each other. The real complementary split is disciplinary, not oppositional: Watson supplies the technical/military texture (missile specifics, game theory, his own direct Reagan-era technical postings), while Medish supplies the diplomatic/legal frame (treaty architecture, his own direct experience organizing Clinton-Putin summits). Neither ever pushes back on the other's claims; each mostly extends or adds precision to what the other just said. The one genuinely personal, unscripted moment is Medish's own disclosure that he was physically present, as an infant, at JFK's 1963 American University 'Strategy for Peace' speech — his father received his PhD diploma from Kennedy that same day — offered as lived, not abstract, connection to the arms-control history being discussed. The clearest point of real divergence isn't between the two guests but within each of their own closing answers to Nate's direct question about whether current leadership can rise to this moment: Medish frames himself as 'an optimist... but worried,' holding both at once; Watson draws a sharper line, affirming humanity broadly can meet the challenge while flatly denying that current political leaders specifically can ('can our leaders meet these challenges? ...my answer is no, they can't'), attributing this to a real structural mismatch between the personality traits that win elections and the self-doubting, revision-willing traits good decision-making actually requires."@en .

<http://example.org/tgs#PanelDiscussion.RR_22_CouldTheWestLoseTheResourceWars> a owl:NamedIndividual ;
    <http://example.org/thinkr#discusses> <http://example.org/tgs#Concept.GrowthImperative> ;
    <http://example.org/thinkr#hasCanonicalSource> <https://www.thegreatsimplification.com/episode/reality-roundtable-22> ;
    <http://example.org/thinkr#hasEpisodeSegment> <http://example.org/tgs#EpisodeSegment.RealityRoundtable> ;
    <http://example.org/thinkr#hasEpisodeType> <http://example.org/thinkr#EpisodeType.PanelDiscussion> ;
    <http://example.org/thinkr#hasGuest> <http://example.org/tgs#Persona.CraigTindale> ,
    <http://example.org/tgs#Persona.MichaelEvery> ;
    <http://example.org/thinkr#hasHost> <http://example.org/tgs#Persona.NateHagens> ;
    <http://example.org/thinkr#hasTranscript> <https://www.thegreatsimplification.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/03/RR-22-Transcript-Could-the-West-Lose-the-Resource-Wars_.pdf> ;
    <http://purl.org/dc/terms/contributor> <http://example.org/tgs#Persona.CraigTindale> ,
    <http://example.org/tgs#Persona.MichaelEvery> ;
    <http://purl.org/dc/terms/creator> <http://example.org/tgs#Persona.NateHagens> ;
    <http://purl.org/dc/terms/source> <https://www.thegreatsimplification.com/episode/reality-roundtable-22> ;
    <http://example.org/thinkr#hasDiscussionSummary> "Framed by Nate himself at the outset as two people who had been independently 'telling parallel stories' finally merging them — and the conversation lives up to that framing, with Tindale and Every remarkably aligned throughout rather than debating. The real complementary split is textural, not oppositional: Tindale supplies ground-level industrial/supply-chain detail (his own 'golden screw' concept — a single missing input that halts an entire system — and a two-ledger financial-versus-material framework), while Every supplies the macro geopolitical-strategy vocabulary ('economic statecraft,' pointed comparisons of US and Chinese industrial policy). Each consistently extends the other's point rather than qualifying it; Every at one point simply says 'I concur' before adding his own layer. The one moment either explicitly flags real disagreement is telling precisely because it's so rare: Every says outright 'this is the first time I differ from Craig at all,' pushing back on Tindale's skepticism that AI job losses will be modest — arguing that even a much smaller disruption (8%, not the touted 80%) could be politically destabilizing given how radicalized and polarized the public already is. Nate's own role is unusually reflective for an interview host — he calls this 'probably one of the most intense conversations' of the podcast's four-year run, and twice interrupts the material density with genuinely human asides (a girlfriend's text about adopting a puppy, arriving mid-Tindale's point about elder care). The episode closes not on analysis but on personal practice: Tindale describes growing and canning his own food as a direct, literal response to the very systemic fragility he spent the previous ninety minutes diagnosing."@en .

<http://example.org/tgs#PanelDiscussion.RR_23_ThePlasticDetox> a owl:NamedIndividual ;
    <http://example.org/thinkr#discusses> <http://example.org/tgs#Concept.CarbonPulse> ,
    <http://example.org/tgs#Concept.Superorganism> ;
    <http://example.org/thinkr#hasCanonicalSource> <https://www.thegreatsimplification.com/episode/reality-roundtable-23> ;
    <http://example.org/thinkr#hasEpisodeSegment> <http://example.org/tgs#EpisodeSegment.RealityRoundtable> ;
    <http://example.org/thinkr#hasEpisodeType> <http://example.org/thinkr#EpisodeType.PanelDiscussion> ;
    <http://example.org/thinkr#hasGuest> <http://example.org/tgs#Persona.ShannaSwan> ,
    <http://example.org/tgs#Persona.SianSutherland> ;
    <http://example.org/thinkr#hasHost> <http://example.org/tgs#Persona.NateHagens> ;
    <http://example.org/thinkr#hasTranscript> <https://www.thegreatsimplification.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/03/RR-23-Transcript-The-Plastic-Detox.pdf> ;
    <http://purl.org/dc/terms/contributor> <http://example.org/tgs#Persona.ShannaSwan> ,
    <http://example.org/tgs#Persona.SianSutherland> ;
    <http://purl.org/dc/terms/creator> <http://example.org/tgs#Persona.NateHagens> ;
    <http://purl.org/dc/terms/source> <https://www.thegreatsimplification.com/episode/reality-roundtable-23> ;
    <http://example.org/thinkr#hasDiscussionSummary> "Warm and deeply collegial throughout — Swan and Sutherland are established real-world colleagues (co-present at UN Plastics Treaty negotiations) as well as friends, and Nate's own opening explicitly names the shift from 'meeting you two as activists and scientists' to being actual friends after four-plus years. The complementary split is expertise-based, not oppositional: Swan supplies the clinical/epidemiological data (urinary metabolites, sperm counts, the documentary's own specific intervention protocol), while Sutherland supplies industry and policy strategy (the 'cattle prod and lightning rod' framing for A Plastic Planet's work, the Plastic Free Babies campaign). One moment stands out as genuinely different from every other RR built this session: asked directly for the documentary's central result — how many of the six couples became pregnant — Swan deliberately declines to answer, wanting viewers to watch the film rather than get a spoiler, and Sutherland backs her ('no spoilers from Shanna'). This isn't evasion in the sense of avoiding a hard question; it's a real, distinct interactional dynamic — a promotional and narrative consideration overriding an otherwise-answered pattern of direct scientific replies throughout the rest of the episode. The other genuinely honest moment comes when Nate raises a deliberately uncomfortable question — whether awareness of these interventions could become an 'evolutionary filter' where only the informed and wealthy can afford to reduce their exposure and successfully reproduce — and Swan responds with real epistemic humility rather than a rehearsed answer: 'I think it sounds brilliant and I don't have any response.' The closest thing to a substantive correction comes from Nate himself at the close, gently but directly qualifying Sutherland's framing that 'we' collectively care about the environment: 'We do care about the environment. The system doesn't care' — offered as an addition to her point rather than a rebuttal, but a real distinction he clearly wanted on the record."@en .

<http://example.org/tgs#PanelDiscussion.RR_24_TheFantasyOfSpaceColonization> a owl:NamedIndividual ;
    <http://example.org/thinkr#discusses> <http://example.org/tgs#Concept.CarbonPulse> ,
    <http://example.org/tgs#Concept.EnergyReturnOnInvestment> ,
    <http://example.org/tgs#SchoolOfThought.TechnoOptimism> ;
    <http://example.org/thinkr#hasCanonicalSource> <https://www.thegreatsimplification.com/episode/reality-roundtable-24> ;
    <http://example.org/thinkr#hasEpisodeSegment> <http://example.org/tgs#EpisodeSegment.RealityRoundtable> ;
    <http://example.org/thinkr#hasEpisodeType> <http://example.org/thinkr#EpisodeType.PanelDiscussion> ;
    <http://example.org/thinkr#hasGuest> <http://example.org/tgs#Persona.DJWhite> ,
    <http://example.org/tgs#Persona.TomMurphy> ;
    <http://example.org/thinkr#hasHost> <http://example.org/tgs#Persona.NateHagens> ;
    <http://example.org/thinkr#hasTranscript> <https://www.thegreatsimplification.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/04/RR-24-Transcript-The-Fantasy-of-Space-Colonization.pdf> ;
    <http://purl.org/dc/terms/contributor> <http://example.org/tgs#Persona.DJWhite> ,
    <http://example.org/tgs#Persona.TomMurphy> ;
    <http://purl.org/dc/terms/creator> <http://example.org/tgs#Persona.NateHagens> ;
    <http://purl.org/dc/terms/source> <https://www.thegreatsimplification.com/episode/reality-roundtable-24> ;
    <http://example.org/thinkr#hasDiscussionSummary> "Even more uniformly convergent than RR-1 — Tom Murphy and DJ White, ~20-year friends, never actually disagree with each other anywhere in the transcript; they build on and extend each other's points throughout, arriving from two complementary but distinct disciplinary angles rather than debating one. Murphy supplies the hard biophysical case (asteroid-mining economics that work out to roughly $130,000/ounce for plain dirt; radiation doses that make a lifetime in space a near-guaranteed early cancer death; the rocket equation implying an asteroid-return mission would need several asteroids' worth of fuel), while White supplies the ecological/consciousness case (his own dolphin-cognition and Greenpeace background, arguing the 'real profound stuff' — minds we can already talk to and are actively driving extinct — is here on Earth, not hypothetically out there). Both independently and repeatedly frame techno-optimism as displaced religion (AI as new gods, uploading as immortality, Dyson spheres as cathedrals), not as a rival scientific claim worth adjudicating on the merits. The one place real friction appears is not between the two guests but against the absent, unnamed techno-optimist position itself, which Nate keeps them pointed at with pointed, sometimes deliberately provocative questions (invoking Matt Damon's potatoes in The Martian, asking directly why two of 'the smartest people' he knows hold a minority view). The episode closes on a register shift from technical to personal — White's story of comforting a dying baby humpback whale, Murphy's closing 'talk to animals' — both converging one final time on the same point: the wonder people project onto space is already available here, and is being destroyed."@en .

<http://example.org/tgs#PanelDiscussion.RR_25_WhyStandardizedTestingContributedToTheMetacrisis> a owl:NamedIndividual ;
    <http://example.org/thinkr#discusses> <http://example.org/tgs#Concept.Superorganism> ;
    <http://example.org/thinkr#hasCanonicalSource> <https://www.thegreatsimplification.com/episode/reality-roundtable-25> ;
    <http://example.org/thinkr#hasEpisodeSegment> <http://example.org/tgs#EpisodeSegment.RealityRoundtable> ;
    <http://example.org/thinkr#hasEpisodeType> <http://example.org/thinkr#EpisodeType.PanelDiscussion> ;
    <http://example.org/thinkr#hasGuest> <http://example.org/tgs#Persona.TheoDawson> ,
    <http://example.org/tgs#Persona.ZakStein> ;
    <http://example.org/thinkr#hasHost> <http://example.org/tgs#Persona.NateHagens> ;
    <http://example.org/thinkr#hasTranscript> <https://www.thegreatsimplification.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/05/RR-25-Transcript-Learning-in-a-Way-that-Actually-Matters.pdf> ;
    <http://purl.org/dc/terms/contributor> <http://example.org/tgs#Persona.TheoDawson> ,
    <http://example.org/tgs#Persona.ZakStein> ;
    <http://purl.org/dc/terms/creator> <http://example.org/tgs#Persona.NateHagens> ;
    <http://purl.org/dc/terms/source> <https://www.thegreatsimplification.com/episode/reality-roundtable-25> ;
    <http://example.org/thinkr#hasDiscussionSummary> "Dawson and Stein are not merely co-guests but established real academic collaborators — they co-published together as early as 2004, and Stein is himself a co-founder of Dawson's organization, Lectica. That history shows throughout: Stein consistently defers to Dawson on technical measurement detail while supplying the broader philosophical and civilizational framing, and at one point directly credits her as the origin of his own thinking ('the first part of my career was working with Theo'). The episode's central intellectual move is Dawson's own sharp distinction between 'testing' (what nearly all of 20th-century education actually did) and 'measurement' (a rarer, content-independent standard she argues her own Lectical Assessment System achieves) — a distinction she returns to repeatedly as the conversation's organizing frame. Nate draws an explicit bridge between the guests' education focus and his own usual energy/systems framing, asking Stein directly to connect Lectica's work to 'the broader meta-crisis' — Stein answers by invoking his own paper, 'Education is the Metacrisis,' and reframing civilizational purpose itself as a choice between accumulating abstract profit and regenerating the next generation's capacity. The one small, genuinely human moment is Stein's own self-deprecating aside about his dyslexia while trying to recall a historical name mid-sentence. Dawson's own most personal disclosure — living off-grid with her children for a significant stretch, and the jolt of re-encountering advertising and its effect on her self-image afterward — grounds the episode's abstract argument about embodied learning in a real, lived account rather than only theory. Nate closes by revealing the episode's own origin: a private, 1.5-hour conversation between him and Stein about AI and education that led directly to inviting Dawson onto the show."@en .

<http://example.org/tgs#PanelDiscussion.RR_26_WhyCommunityFails> a owl:NamedIndividual ;
    <http://example.org/thinkr#discusses> <http://example.org/tgs#Concept.Amonging> ,
    <http://example.org/tgs#Concept.Communing> ,
    <http://example.org/tgs#Concept.DarkTriad> ,
    <http://example.org/tgs#Concept.DunbarsNumber> ,
    <http://example.org/tgs#Concept.Metacrisis> ,
    <http://example.org/tgs#Concept.RadicalHospitality> ,
    <http://example.org/tgs#Concept.Schismogenesis> ,
    <http://example.org/tgs#Concept.Superorganism> ,
    <http://example.org/tgs#Concept.Symmathesy> ,
    <http://example.org/tgs#SchoolOfThought.WarmData> ;
    <http://example.org/thinkr#hasCanonicalSource> <https://www.thegreatsimplification.com/episode/reality-roundtable-26> ;
    <http://example.org/thinkr#hasEpisodeSegment> <http://example.org/tgs#EpisodeSegment.RealityRoundtable> ;
    <http://example.org/thinkr#hasEpisodeType> <http://example.org/thinkr#EpisodeType.PanelDiscussion> ;
    <http://example.org/thinkr#hasGuest> <http://example.org/tgs#Persona.JonathanGoldsmith> ,
    <http://example.org/tgs#Persona.LucasJackson> ,
    <http://example.org/tgs#Persona.NoraBateson> ;
    <http://example.org/thinkr#hasHost> <http://example.org/tgs#Persona.NateHagens> ;
    <http://purl.org/dc/terms/contributor> <http://example.org/tgs#Persona.JonathanGoldsmith> ,
    <http://example.org/tgs#Persona.LucasJackson> ,
    <http://example.org/tgs#Persona.NoraBateson> ;
    <http://purl.org/dc/terms/creator> <http://example.org/tgs#Persona.NateHagens> ;
    <http://purl.org/dc/terms/source> <https://natehagens.substack.com/p/why-community-fails-everyone-wants> ,
    <https://www.thegreatsimplification.com/episode/reality-roundtable-26> ;
    <http://example.org/thinkr#hasDiscussionSummary> "Different in character from RR-1 and RR-24's convergence-through-complementary-expertise — here the three guests are colleagues at the same institute, with Nora as the clear intellectual center (founder, coiner of the terms being discussed) and Jonathan and Lucas mostly contributing illustrative, experiential texture (a son's skating community, tea-making, a shared meal ritual, a teenage trip to see the Dalai Lama) rather than distinct or competing positions. What makes this episode distinct is that the conversation doesn't just describe the practice it's about — unscripted, non-competitive, deeply attentive listening — it visibly performs it; Nate explicitly notices this happening in real time more than once, remarking that none of the guests give him an immediate reflexive answer and that the best parts of the conversation were when he 'forgot I was a podcast host.' The closest thing to genuine friction is Nate's own pointed challenge — if a community selects for openness, curiosity, integrity, and generosity, wouldn't it simply get exploited or outcompeted by people with Dark Triad traits? — which the three guests work through collaboratively rather than by disagreeing with each other, building out Jonathan's 'lattice work' image together (bad-faith actors can't get purchase because the group's structure keeps moving). Nate also brings real personal vulnerability into the conversation himself, using his own unresolved dishes-with-a-partner example, which Nora then uses live to illustrate 'systems holdback' (a form of schismogenesis) rather than treating it as a hypothetical. The episode closes with two real, direct corroborations of facts already in this graph: Nora naming her new book, Belly, and confirming that she's appeared on the show more than anyone except Daniel Schmachtenberger."@en .

<http://example.org/tgs#PanelDiscussion.RR_27_AnEcologicalPsychology> a owl:NamedIndividual ;
    <http://example.org/thinkr#discusses> <http://example.org/tgs#Concept.DarkTriad> ,
    <http://example.org/tgs#Concept.GrowthImperative> ,
    <http://example.org/tgs#Concept.Superorganism> ;
    <http://example.org/thinkr#hasCanonicalSource> <https://www.thegreatsimplification.com/episode/reality-roundtable-27> ;
    <http://example.org/thinkr#hasEpisodeSegment> <http://example.org/tgs#EpisodeSegment.RealityRoundtable> ;
    <http://example.org/thinkr#hasEpisodeType> <http://example.org/thinkr#EpisodeType.PanelDiscussion> ;
    <http://example.org/thinkr#hasGuest> <http://example.org/tgs#Persona.RichardSchwartz> ,
    <http://example.org/tgs#Persona.SteffiBednarek> ;
    <http://example.org/thinkr#hasHost> <http://example.org/tgs#Persona.NateHagens> ;
    <http://example.org/thinkr#hasTranscript> <https://www.thegreatsimplification.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/08/RR-27-Transcript-An-Ecological-Psychology.pdf> ;
    <http://purl.org/dc/terms/contributor> <http://example.org/tgs#Persona.RichardSchwartz> ,
    <http://example.org/tgs#Persona.SteffiBednarek> ;
    <http://purl.org/dc/terms/creator> <http://example.org/tgs#Persona.NateHagens> ;
    <http://purl.org/dc/terms/source> <https://www.thegreatsimplification.com/episode/reality-roundtable-27> ;
    <http://example.org/thinkr#hasDiscussionSummary> "The longest and most extended conversation among all built RRs — Nate's own words near the close: 'coming up on two hours, this is one of the longer conversations I've had this year.' Schwartz and Bednarek are visibly not just co-guests but active collaborators already deep in an ongoing dialogue of their own ('this is a conversation that I am currently having with Dick and I really love it,' Bednarek says at one point) — the complementary split is genuinely structural rather than just topical: Schwartz supplies the clinical, individual-level IFS framework directly, while Bednarek supplies its real extension into organizations, governments, and whole cultures via her own Systems Wayfinding work. The one moment of real, pointed challenge in the episode comes from Nate himself, not between the guests — he directly names the skepticism his own audience is likely to feel toward psychological frameworks given the podcast's usual material/energy/systems focus, asking bluntly how IFS could actually affect a trajectory driven by biophysical limits rather than individual psychology. Both guests take the challenge seriously rather than deflecting: Bednarek is explicit that 'no psychological approach can stop the unfolding of biodiversity loss that is already underway,' reframing the value as being about how humans meet what's coming rather than preventing it; Schwartz answers instead with his own long-term, admittedly 'grandiose' goal of IFS replacing the dominant paradigm for relating to the mind entirely. The episode's most candid moment is Schwartz's own disclosed intellectual and spiritual evolution, told live rather than summarized: he began the work as a self-described 'scientific atheist' and has only this year become openly willing to describe the IFS 'Self' as a 'particle state' of something larger that different spiritual traditions have independently pointed to under different names. Nate's own participation is unusually reflexive throughout — he explicitly narrates applying the framework to himself in real time ('I feel like I've been in self-energy since this conversation started'), and Schwartz risks a genuinely vulnerable, self-deprecating aside about being triggered by his own wife, fully aware she would later watch the recording."@en .

<http://example.org/tgs#PanelDiscussion.RR_2_DeeperEcology> a owl:NamedIndividual ;
    <http://example.org/thinkr#discusses> <http://example.org/tgs#Concept.EcologicalFootprint> ,
    <http://example.org/tgs#Concept.LimitsToGrowth> ,
    <http://example.org/tgs#Concept.Overshoot> ;
    <http://example.org/thinkr#hasCanonicalSource> <https://www.thegreatsimplification.com/episode/rr02-bateson-rees-weyler> ;
    <http://example.org/thinkr#hasEpisodeSegment> <http://example.org/tgs#EpisodeSegment.RealityRoundtable> ;
    <http://example.org/thinkr#hasEpisodeType> <http://example.org/thinkr#EpisodeType.PanelDiscussion> ;
    <http://example.org/thinkr#hasGuest> <http://example.org/tgs#Persona.NoraBateson> ,
    <http://example.org/tgs#Persona.RexWeyler> ,
    <http://example.org/tgs#Persona.WilliamRees> ;
    <http://example.org/thinkr#hasHost> <http://example.org/tgs#Persona.NateHagens> ;
    <http://example.org/thinkr#hasTranscript> <https://www.thegreatsimplification.com/wp-content/uploads/2023/07/BatesonReesWeyler.pdf> ;
    <http://purl.org/dc/terms/contributor> <http://example.org/tgs#Persona.NoraBateson> ,
    <http://example.org/tgs#Persona.RexWeyler> ,
    <http://example.org/tgs#Persona.WilliamRees> ;
    <http://purl.org/dc/terms/creator> <http://example.org/tgs#Persona.NateHagens> ;
    <http://purl.org/dc/terms/source> <https://www.thegreatsimplification.com/episode/rr02-bateson-rees-weyler> ;
    <http://example.org/thinkr#hasDiscussionSummary> "The most substantively adversarial of the built RRs so far, though still warmly collegial — Nate's own opening explicitly names the contrast: 'people agree on deep ecology and the ecological predicament of human overshoot, yet take wildly different perspectives in how they view it.' Rees supplies the systems-scientific backbone (dissipative structures, thermodynamics, the mathematics of overshoot-and-crash), Weyler supplies historical/movement context and a harder moral edge (open frustration that environmental groups avoid words like 'overshoot'), and Bateson consistently redirects both toward relational process over prescription ('who can you be when you're with me' versus any engineered solution). The clearest real disagreement in any built RR happens here: when Rees says he'd prefer 1-2 billion people living well to 10 billion living in poverty, Nate directly pushes back ('I actually disagree... I think that's the default that we're headed towards'), and Rees concedes the practical point while holding his normative preference ('I agree it's easier, but I said IF you want to plan for something'). Bateson explicitly declines to fully endorse the contraction-is-inevitable framing Rees and Weyler share, prompting Nate to ask her directly whether she agrees — a rare moment where a built RR's host names a real, unresolved difference among guests rather than treating the conversation as settled consensus. Population and eugenics form the other real point of productive friction: Weyler raises population decline directly and names the taboo around it, Bateson immediately reframes the same territory through care and relational support rather than numbers, explicitly naming the eugenics risk in framing it any other way — not a rebuttal of Weyler's point, but a genuine redirection of its terms that he doesn't contest."@en .

<http://example.org/tgs#PanelDiscussion.RR_3_UnlearningEconomics> a owl:NamedIndividual ;
    <http://example.org/thinkr#discusses> <http://dbpedia.org/resource/Planetary_boundaries> ,
    <http://dbpedia.org/resource/Doughnut_(economic_model)> ,
    <http://example.org/tgs#Concept.EnergyReturnOnInvestment> ,
    <http://example.org/tgs#SchoolOfThought.DoughnutEconomics> ,
    <http://example.org/tgs#SchoolOfThought.PlanetaryBoundaries> ;
    <http://example.org/thinkr#hasCanonicalSource> <https://www.thegreatsimplification.com/episode/rr03-erickson-farley-raworth-keen> ;
    <http://example.org/thinkr#hasEpisodeSegment> <http://example.org/tgs#EpisodeSegment.RealityRoundtable> ;
    <http://example.org/thinkr#hasEpisodeType> <http://example.org/thinkr#EpisodeType.PanelDiscussion> ;
    <http://example.org/thinkr#hasGuest> <http://example.org/tgs#Persona.JonErickson> ,
    <http://example.org/tgs#Persona.JoshFarley> ,
    <http://example.org/tgs#Persona.KateRaworth> ,
    <http://example.org/tgs#Persona.SteveKeen> ;
    <http://example.org/thinkr#hasHost> <http://example.org/tgs#Persona.NateHagens> ;
    <http://example.org/thinkr#hasTranscript> <https://www.thegreatsimplification.com/wp-content/uploads/2023/08/RR03EricksonFarleyRaworthKeenTranscript.docx.pdf> ;
    <http://purl.org/dc/terms/contributor> <http://example.org/tgs#Persona.JonErickson> ,
    <http://example.org/tgs#Persona.JoshFarley> ,
    <http://example.org/tgs#Persona.KateRaworth> ,
    <http://example.org/tgs#Persona.SteveKeen> ;
    <http://purl.org/dc/terms/creator> <http://example.org/tgs#Persona.NateHagens> ;
    <http://purl.org/dc/terms/source> <https://www.thegreatsimplification.com/episode/rr03-erickson-farley-raworth-keen> ;
    <http://example.org/thinkr#hasDiscussionSummary> "The most substantively adversarial-yet-collegial of the built RRs — the only one where two different guests explicitly announce they're playing 'devil's advocate' against a co-panelist's argument, not just adding texture. Raworth does it twice: first pressing Erickson on what actually replaces the rational-actor model once you've torn it down ('then what?'), and later challenging Farley's rationing proposal directly, arguing markets plus redistribution and a universal basic income would work better than rationing given rationing's own real historical failure modes (black markets, Soviet-style central planning). Farley doesn't concede — he counters with a specific alternative (guaranteed access to essential goods specifically, not a general UBI, because essential-good prices are too volatile for a fixed income to track), and the two continue trading specifics rather than one yielding. Keen plays devil's advocate 'in a different direction,' agreeing entirely with Erickson's critique of neoclassical economics but pushing further — arguing the theory fails on its own internal mathematical terms even before you get to its empirical failures. The clearest real disagreement, though, isn't among the guests but in how each answers Nate's own closing question — can any of this actually be taught at scale? Keen is bluntly pessimistic ('I don't think they can'), Farley says he 'sadly agrees a little bit' but still points to concrete, working programs, and Raworth explicitly frames herself as 'leaning into possibility,' citing a real curriculum win (the Doughnut now appearing in an International Baccalaureate economics textbook) as evidence change is already happening, however slowly. All four converge without disagreement on Raworth's own closing 'Embedded Economy' diagram — Erickson calls it 'not rocket science' as a genuine compliment, and Raworth explicitly welcomes that as the whole point."@en .

<http://example.org/tgs#PanelDiscussion.RR_4_TitanicOceans> a owl:NamedIndividual ;
    <http://example.org/thinkr#discusses> <http://example.org/tgs#EarthSystemComponent.AMOC> ;
    <http://example.org/thinkr#hasCanonicalSource> <https://www.thegreatsimplification.com/episode/rr04-pauly-turiel-ward> ;
    <http://example.org/thinkr#hasEpisodeSegment> <http://example.org/tgs#EpisodeSegment.RealityRoundtable> ;
    <http://example.org/thinkr#hasEpisodeType> <http://example.org/thinkr#EpisodeType.PanelDiscussion> ;
    <http://example.org/thinkr#hasGuest> <http://example.org/tgs#Persona.AntonioTuriel> ,
    <http://example.org/tgs#Persona.DanielPauly> ,
    <http://example.org/tgs#Persona.PeterWard> ;
    <http://example.org/thinkr#hasHost> <http://example.org/tgs#Persona.NateHagens> ;
    <http://example.org/thinkr#hasTranscript> <https://www.thegreatsimplification.com/wp-content/uploads/2023/09/RR04PaulyTurielWard.docx.pdf> ;
    <http://purl.org/dc/terms/contributor> <http://example.org/tgs#Persona.AntonioTuriel> ,
    <http://example.org/tgs#Persona.DanielPauly> ,
    <http://example.org/tgs#Persona.PeterWard> ;
    <http://purl.org/dc/terms/creator> <http://example.org/tgs#Persona.NateHagens> ;
    <http://purl.org/dc/terms/source> <https://www.thegreatsimplification.com/episode/rr04-pauly-turiel-ward> ;
    <http://example.org/thinkr#hasDiscussionSummary> "Uniformly convergent and unusually warm — three genuine friends of Nate's, each bringing a different real disciplinary lens (Pauly's fisheries/subsidy economics, Turiel's physical oceanography, Ward's deep-time paleobiology) that consistently reinforce rather than compete with each other. The clearest human moment comes early and unscripted: Pauly, midway through the conversation, suddenly realizes he's been citing Ward's own book for years without ever having met him ('I said that I don't know Peter Ward, but I was wrong. I use your book all the time... I just didn't connect at all') — a small-world moment neither anticipated. Ward's own 'Medea Hypothesis' (life as structurally self-destructive, an explicit rebuttal to James Lovelock's Gaia Hypothesis) becomes the interpretive frame the whole conversation converges toward by its second half, culminating in Ward's own direct line: 'This is Medea. This is not Gaia... This is life doing it to itself.' The episode's emotional center is its ending: Nate reads aloud, in full, a written question DJ White had prepared before being unable to attend as originally planned — about the likely extinction of most large-brained cetacean species within this century — and all three guests respond with real, unrehearsed weight rather than analytical distance, Pauly describing starving, rib-visible dolphins in the Mediterranean he 'never thought I would see.' Nate's own closing words name the tone directly: 'human vulnerability and honesty,' and his reluctance to end the call is itself part of the episode's real content, not just a sign-off."@en .

<http://example.org/tgs#PanelDiscussion.RR_5_FoodAndCommunityInTheRuins> a owl:NamedIndividual ;
    <http://example.org/thinkr#hasCanonicalSource> <https://www.thegreatsimplification.com/episode/rr05-hine-smaje-thiel> ;
    <http://example.org/thinkr#hasEpisodeSegment> <http://example.org/tgs#EpisodeSegment.RealityRoundtable> ;
    <http://example.org/thinkr#hasEpisodeType> <http://example.org/thinkr#EpisodeType.PanelDiscussion> ;
    <http://example.org/thinkr#hasGuest> <http://example.org/tgs#Persona.ChrisSmaje> ,
    <http://example.org/tgs#Persona.DougaldHine> ,
    <http://example.org/tgs#Persona.PellaThiel> ;
    <http://example.org/thinkr#hasHost> <http://example.org/tgs#Persona.NateHagens> ;
    <http://example.org/thinkr#hasTranscript> <https://www.thegreatsimplification.com/wp-content/uploads/2023/10/RR05Transcript.docx.pdf> ;
    <http://purl.org/dc/terms/contributor> <http://example.org/tgs#Persona.ChrisSmaje> ,
    <http://example.org/tgs#Persona.DougaldHine> ,
    <http://example.org/tgs#Persona.PellaThiel> ;
    <http://purl.org/dc/terms/creator> <http://example.org/tgs#Persona.NateHagens> ;
    <http://purl.org/dc/terms/source> <https://www.thegreatsimplification.com/episode/rr05-hine-smaje-thiel> ;
    <http://example.org/thinkr#hasDiscussionSummary> "Uniformly warm and complementary, distinct in one real way from most other built RRs: the three guests were already personal friends before this recording (Nate had stayed at Pella Thiel's farm helping with lambing the night before, and had met both her and Hine in person in Sweden weeks earlier), so the conversation reads less like three experts meeting for the first time and more like a continuation of an existing relationship. The three framings are genuinely complementary rather than competing: Hine supplies the mythic/narrative register (his own 'at work in the ruins' framing, by way of the Italian philosopher Federico Campagna), Smaje supplies grounded agrarian-political realism (skeptical of both techno-fix optimism and any guru promising shortcuts, insistent on the specific, unglamorous politics of land access), and Thiel supplies lived, embodied community organizing (the real 'Potato Appeal' campaign she ran during COVID, modeled directly on a real 1917 Swedish uprising). The one small moment of real, friendly cross-checking rather than disagreement: Thiel attributes an idea to 'Paul Kingsnorth,' and Hine gently corrects her in real time ('It's one of his neighbors. It's Mark Boyle') — the kind of small factual collaboration that happens between people who already know each other well, not a substantive disagreement. Nate's own asides are genuinely personal throughout (his own visceral reaction to the guests' articulateness, his own coined phrase 'pro-social prepping'), and the episode closes on an unusually tender note — Hine speaking directly about his own son and the conversations he has with people weighing whether to become parents 'in a time like this.'"@en .

<http://example.org/tgs#PanelDiscussion.RR_6_FossilFreeFoodSystems> a owl:NamedIndividual ;
    <http://example.org/thinkr#hasCanonicalSource> <https://www.thegreatsimplification.com/episode/rr06-bradford-millison-shiva-zetah> ;
    <http://example.org/thinkr#hasEpisodeSegment> <http://example.org/tgs#EpisodeSegment.RealityRoundtable> ;
    <http://example.org/thinkr#hasEpisodeType> <http://example.org/thinkr#EpisodeType.PanelDiscussion> ;
    <http://example.org/thinkr#hasGuest> <http://example.org/tgs#Persona.AndrewMillison> ,
    <http://example.org/tgs#Persona.DanielZetah> ,
    <http://example.org/tgs#Persona.JasonBradford> ,
    <http://example.org/tgs#Persona.VandanaShiva> ;
    <http://example.org/thinkr#hasHost> <http://example.org/tgs#Persona.NateHagens> ;
    <http://example.org/thinkr#hasTranscript> <https://www.thegreatsimplification.com/wp-content/uploads/2023/11/RR06BradfordMillisonShivaZetahTranscript.docx.pdf> ;
    <http://purl.org/dc/terms/contributor> <http://example.org/tgs#Persona.AndrewMillison> ,
    <http://example.org/tgs#Persona.DanielZetah> ,
    <http://example.org/tgs#Persona.JasonBradford> ,
    <http://example.org/tgs#Persona.VandanaShiva> ;
    <http://purl.org/dc/terms/creator> <http://example.org/tgs#Persona.NateHagens> ;
    <http://purl.org/dc/terms/source> <https://www.thegreatsimplification.com/episode/rr06-bradford-millison-shiva-zetah> ;
    <http://example.org/thinkr#hasDiscussionSummary> "Highly convergent — Jason Bradford's own mid-episode summary names it directly: 'I think we're all kind of singing the same song here.' The one moment of genuine, explicit reframing rather than agreement comes from Daniel Zetah, who opens his turn by directly challenging Nate's own question: 'I would actually like to just challenge the question itself' — arguing the premise that the current fossil-fueled system is even 'feeding the world' well is itself false, given documented malnutrition and obesity coexisting under it. A second, milder real disagreement is one of degree rather than direction: Bradford's opening statement calls for depopulating cities outright, while Millison pushes back gently that 'not all cities are created equal,' pointing to cities historically sited on rivers and trade routes as potentially viable in a lower-energy future rather than uniformly abandoned. Shiva grounds the whole conversation in real, specific longitudinal data from her own farm (20 years of soil nitrogen, zinc, and manganese measurements comparing organic and chemical-input plots), giving the discussion an empirical anchor the other three build on rather than dispute. The episode's most self-aware moment is Bradford's own 'Shire' analogy via Lord of the Rings, which he flags himself as 'corny' before continuing anyway — Nate affectionately teases him for it at the close ('I knew you were corny') while praising his 'agro-ecological wisdom.' All four converge fully, without qualification, on ending agricultural subsidies and patents on seed/life as the necessary first policy steps — the clearest full consensus point in the episode."@en .

<http://example.org/tgs#PanelDiscussion.RR_7_PovertyBlind> a owl:NamedIndividual ;
    <http://example.org/thinkr#hasCanonicalSource> <https://www.thegreatsimplification.com/episode/rr07-hoopes-kilde-perry-sulamoyo> ;
    <http://example.org/thinkr#hasEpisodeSegment> <http://example.org/tgs#EpisodeSegment.RealityRoundtable> ;
    <http://example.org/thinkr#hasEpisodeType> <http://example.org/thinkr#EpisodeType.PanelDiscussion> ;
    <http://example.org/thinkr#hasGuest> <http://example.org/tgs#Persona.DalitsoSulamoyo> ,
    <http://example.org/tgs#Persona.MarcPerry> ,
    <http://example.org/tgs#Persona.PeterKilde> ,
    <http://example.org/tgs#Persona.StephanieHoopes> ;
    <http://example.org/thinkr#hasHost> <http://example.org/tgs#Persona.NateHagens> ;
    <http://example.org/thinkr#hasTranscript> <https://www.thegreatsimplification.com/wp-content/uploads/2023/12/RR07HoopesKildePerryandSulamoyoTranscript.docx.pdf> ;
    <http://purl.org/dc/terms/contributor> <http://example.org/tgs#Persona.DalitsoSulamoyo> ,
    <http://example.org/tgs#Persona.MarcPerry> ,
    <http://example.org/tgs#Persona.PeterKilde> ,
    <http://example.org/tgs#Persona.StephanieHoopes> ;
    <http://purl.org/dc/terms/creator> <http://example.org/tgs#Persona.NateHagens> ;
    <http://purl.org/dc/terms/source> <https://www.thegreatsimplification.com/episode/rr07-hoopes-kilde-perry-sulamoyo> ;
    <http://example.org/thinkr#hasDiscussionSummary> "Highly collaborative among four practitioners who mostly extend rather than contest each other's points, anchored throughout by Hoopes's own ALICE data (41% of US households below a survival budget despite employment). The clearest moment of real, pointed directness rather than diplomatic framing comes from Marc Perry, describing the frustration in Black communities at having raised the same structural concerns for decades to what he calls 'deaf ears' — 'it took somebody white to say it in order for you to actually pay attention to it.' A second genuinely candid moment, flagged by the speaker himself as sensitive, is Dalitso Sulamoyo raising real community tension over resource allocation to recent asylum seekers versus longtime residents — he calls it 'quite a touchy issue' and does not soften it further. Peter Kilde grounds the episode's abstract inequality argument in specific documented history — a detailed comparison of Hurricane Katrina and Hurricane Maria's radically unequal disaster responses, including real death tolls and recovery-fund disparities — building on his own extensive, real personal connection to New Orleans and a professional history with peak-oil and resource-depletion work (ASPO-USA board membership) that predates and directly parallels Nate's own subject matter. Marc Perry's own personal disclosure — growing up materially poor but 'rich' in family and community, not realizing he was poor until told — offers a lived counterpoint to the episode's data-heavy first half. The two guests' closing interpretive frames diverge memorably without conflicting: Kilde insists poverty is fundamentally 'a math problem,' not a behavioral one, while Perry frames the culture's complacency through Huxley's Brave New World rather than Orwell's 1984 — people appeased into indifference rather than terrorized into it."@en .

<http://example.org/tgs#PanelDiscussion.RR_8_TheIndicMind> a owl:NamedIndividual ;
    <http://example.org/thinkr#discusses> <http://example.org/tgs#Concept.Superorganism> ;
    <http://example.org/thinkr#hasCanonicalSource> <https://www.thegreatsimplification.com/episode/reality-roundtable-8> ;
    <http://example.org/thinkr#hasEpisodeSegment> <http://example.org/tgs#EpisodeSegment.RealityRoundtable> ;
    <http://example.org/thinkr#hasEpisodeType> <http://example.org/thinkr#EpisodeType.PanelDiscussion> ;
    <http://example.org/thinkr#hasGuest> <http://example.org/tgs#Persona.AbhishekThakore> ,
    <http://example.org/tgs#Persona.KejalSavla> ,
    <http://example.org/tgs#Persona.MohitTrivedi> ;
    <http://example.org/thinkr#hasHost> <http://example.org/tgs#Persona.NateHagens> ;
    <http://example.org/thinkr#hasTranscript> <https://www.thegreatsimplification.com/wp-content/uploads/2024/05/RR08TrivediThakoreSavlaTranscript.pdf> ;
    <http://purl.org/dc/terms/contributor> <http://example.org/tgs#Persona.AbhishekThakore> ,
    <http://example.org/tgs#Persona.KejalSavla> ,
    <http://example.org/tgs#Persona.MohitTrivedi> ;
    <http://purl.org/dc/terms/creator> <http://example.org/tgs#Persona.NateHagens> ;
    <http://purl.org/dc/terms/source> <https://www.thegreatsimplification.com/episode/reality-roundtable-8> ;
    <http://example.org/thinkr#hasDiscussionSummary> "Distinctly different register from most other built RRs — philosophical and spiritual rather than technical, with real harmony among the three guests, who have spent years deliberately building what they call a 'relational field' with each other before ever discussing the metacrisis directly. The central original contribution is the 'Indic mind' itself, developed across the episode as a paradox-holding, relationality-first orientation distinct from Western crisis-response framing — Abhishek's own account of first meeting Nate is telling: they spent four hours talking about relationships and process without once mentioning climate or the metacrisis. The clearest moment of Nate's own assumption being corrected live comes when he directly states his expectation that Indians would feel resentment toward the Global North for disproportionate carbon emissions and climate impact — Abhishek and Mohit both describe something more layered: real awareness of the injustice without it curdling into blame, attributed to a cultural orientation toward self-directed agency rather than externalized grievance. Mohit's own account of living without air conditioning through 37-40°C heat by personal choice — while explicitly declining to 'glorify' the choice or claim it as a virtue — grounds the episode's more abstract philosophical claims in a concrete, lived practice. The one real point of divergence, held without resolution rather than smoothed into agreement, is each guest's relationship to hope: Kejal insists hope 'is not a choice... a stand that you take,' while Mohit says plainly 'hope is [an] illusioned word' and offers no answer for it, preferring confrontation with and embrace of the unknown instead. The episode's most emotionally exposed moment is Abhishek's own closing disclosure — 'it makes me very emotional to be honest' — describing his felt sense of a worldwide, unnamed network of people quietly doing this work, a claim he explicitly frames as testimony rather than argument."@en .

<http://example.org/tgs#PanelDiscussion.RR_9_TheNextGenerationsDilemma> a owl:NamedIndividual ;
    <http://example.org/thinkr#discusses> <http://example.org/tgs#Concept.DistributedAgency> ,
    <http://example.org/tgs#Concept.Superorganism> ;
    <http://example.org/thinkr#hasCanonicalSource> <https://www.thegreatsimplification.com/episode/reality-roundtable-9> ;
    <http://example.org/thinkr#hasEpisodeSegment> <http://example.org/tgs#EpisodeSegment.RealityRoundtable> ;
    <http://example.org/thinkr#hasEpisodeType> <http://example.org/thinkr#EpisodeType.PanelDiscussion> ;
    <http://example.org/thinkr#hasGuest> <http://example.org/tgs#Persona.JamesBranagan> ,
    <http://example.org/tgs#Persona.NatashaLinhart> ,
    <http://example.org/tgs#Persona.PriscillaTrinh> ;
    <http://example.org/thinkr#hasHost> <http://example.org/tgs#Persona.NateHagens> ;
    <http://example.org/thinkr#hasTranscript> <https://www.thegreatsimplification.com/wp-content/uploads/2024/06/RR09BranaganLinhartTrinhTranscript.pdf> ;
    <http://purl.org/dc/terms/contributor> <http://example.org/tgs#Persona.JamesBranagan> ,
    <http://example.org/tgs#Persona.NatashaLinhart> ,
    <http://example.org/tgs#Persona.PriscillaTrinh> ;
    <http://purl.org/dc/terms/creator> <http://example.org/tgs#Persona.NateHagens> ;
    <http://purl.org/dc/terms/source> <https://www.thegreatsimplification.com/episode/reality-roundtable-9> ;
    <http://example.org/thinkr#hasDiscussionSummary> "Distinctly personal and emotionally vulnerable, a real departure in register from most other built RRs — three young guests speaking candidly about their own psychological process of confronting the metacrisis rather than analyzing it from professional distance. Natasha Linhart's account is the most exposed: a real, named 'awareness → grief → acceptance' sequence including explicit disclosure of crying alone and feeling powerless during the grief phase, offered without being smoothed over ('that period really sucked. I don't want to sugarcoat it'). Priscilla Trịnh offers a genuinely different phenomenological texture for a similar process — 'a happy tumble down a hill,' exhilarating rather than purely painful — not a contradiction of Natasha's account so much as a different lived experience of a comparable arc, grounded in her own family history as children of Vietnamese refugees and an intersectional-feminist analytical lens distinct from the other two guests'. The one moment of real, direct pushback against Nate's own question comes from James Branagan, who states plainly that he doesn't observe the intergenerational blame Nate's framing assumed: 'I don't really see it... I don't even think blaming is productive' — reframing the real tension instead as a resource-versus-energy mismatch between generations. A genuine, unresolved difference in practice surfaces around social media: Natasha describes actively leaving it, while Priscilla stays engaged but holds herself to a personal rule against 'clicktivism' and 'rage-farming' — two different relationships to the same tool, not reconciled into one answer. The episode closes on a real continuity detail: Priscilla reveals that after Nate stopped teaching his University of Minnesota course, she and fellow students revived it as a peer-led seminar under a new name, 'What to Do About a Fucked Up World.'"@en .

<http://example.org/tgs#Persona.AbhishekThakore> a owl:NamedIndividual ;
    <http://example.org/thinkr#actsThrough> <http://example.org/tgs#Human.AbhishekThakore> ;
    <http://example.org/thinkr#hasPersonalRelationship> <http://example.org/tgs#Relationship.NateHagens_AbhishekThakore> ;
